window.ROSETTA_SEARCH = [{"u":"3DPrintableFiles.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"3D Printable Files","h":[["Face-Plates","Face Plates"],["Printing-Notes","Printing Notes"],["Power-Supply-Mount","Power Supply Mount"],["Printing-Notes_","Printing Notes"],["Signal-Tower-Relays-Mount","Signal Tower Relays Mount"],["Printing-Notes__","Printing Notes"],["Compatibility-Notes","Compatibility Notes"]],"x":"Face Plates USB HDMI Face Plate Base Box Face Plate Expansion Box Face Plate The face plates for the base box and the expansion box are both in this file. They are on separate plates within the file. REB_Plates_v1.3mf Printing Notes These should be printed with the text down. That gives the best, flattest surface for the plate’s surface which is seen. Power Supply Mount Power Supply Mount This is used to hold the 24V -> USB power supply which is used to power the Raspberry Pi and the Mesa 7i92TH. REB_PowerSupplyMount.3mf Printing Notes This should be printed as shown (with the bottom down). Supports are not needed. Signal Tower Relays Mount Signal Tower Relays Mount This is used to hold the 3 relays used for control of the signal tower. REB_Relay_Mount.3mf A cable tie placed between the top relay and the next one down helps hold all 3 snugly in place. Printing Notes This should be printed as shown (with the bottom down). Supports are not needed. Compatibility Notes They are provided as a 3mf file which is formatted for printing on a Bambu Lab printer with multiple filament support. This enables the text to be embedded into the front as a different color. These were tested on a Bambu Labs P1S with an AMS."},{"u":"AxisMapping.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Axis Mapping (HAL to Hardware)","h":[["Plugs-and-channels-fixed-wiring","Plugs and channels (fixed wiring)"],["Default-roles","Default roles"],["Earlier-8-channel-development-boxes","Earlier 8-channel (development) boxes"],["How-roles-become-LinuxCNC-joints","How roles become LinuxCNC joints"]],"x":"The Rosetta has up to 10 motor plugs: 6 on the base box (channels 00 - 05) and 4 on the optional expansion box (channels 06 - 09). Each plug is wired to one channel , a step generator on that box's Mesa 7i92TH card. The operator chooses which role each channel plays - any of the axes X, Z, U, V, W, A, B, C or the spindles Sp0, Sp1 - on the Axis Selection tab of the Settings program. With both boxes there is one channel for each of the 10 roles. Channels 06 - 09 are only used when Expansion Box - Connected is ticked on the Axis Selection tab (saved as expansion_box in RosettaSet_v1.ini). Without it, those channels are greyed out and any role on them is left out of the configuration. Plugs and channels (fixed wiring) The plugs are laid out with the even numbers on the top row and the odd numbers on the bottom row, the same way they are shown on the Axis Selection tab and on the main panel's Axis Assignments tiles: Box Channels Mesa card Step generators Base box 00 / 02 / 04 hm2_7i92.0 (10.10.10.10) Channels 00 - 05 = hm2_7i92.0.stepgen.00 - .05 01 / 03 / 05 Expansion box 06 / 08 hm2_7i92.1 (10.10.10.11) Channels 06 - 09 = hm2_7i92.1.stepgen.00 - .03 07 / 09 Note that the expansion card numbers its step generators from 00 again: channel 06 is hm2_7i92.1.stepgen.00 , not stepgen.06. See Mesa 7i92 Pin Mapping for the header pins. Default roles These are the roles a new system ships with (from Rosetta_Setup/RosettaSet_v1.ini): Channel 00 01 02 03 04 05 06 07 08 09 Box Base Expansion Role X Z B W Sp0 Sp1 U V A C Type Linear Linear Angular Linear Spindle Spindle Linear Linear Angular Angular Earlier 8-channel (development) boxes An earlier 8-channel box is used as a base box: channels 00 - 05 work, and its other two plugs are not used. On such a box, channels 06 and 07 now mean the expansion box's first two plugs, so a role saved on 06 or 07 (the old default was Sp0 and Sp1 there) is left out until it is moved to a base channel on the Axis Selection tab. The rest of a saved assignment is kept as it was. How roles become LinuxCNC joints Joint numbers are not fixed - they are worked out at every launch by Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py (see Launching Rosetta ): The axis letters in use are numbered 0, 1, 2 ... in the order X, Z, B, U, V, W, A, C , skipping any that aren't on a channel in use. With the default roles and no expansion box that gives X=0, Z=1, B=2, W=3. With the expansion box connected it gives X=0, Z=1, B=2, U=3, V=4, W=5, A=6, C=7. A role is only \"in use\" if its channel is in use - so a role on channels 06 - 09 counts only when the expansion box is connected. The spindles in use become LinuxCNC spindle 0 and 1, in the order Sp0, Sp1. Every HAL net is named after the role, not the channel ( pid.x , x-enable , ...), so it always means \"whichever channel is X right now\". The generator points each role's HAL at its channel's card and step generator. Type is fixed by the letter: X, Z, U, V, W are linear (inches or mm); A, B, C are angular (degrees). Y is not available, because Rosetta.ini sets LATHE = 1. Sp1 can only be used when Sp0 is also used (its speed is a percentage of Sp0's). The same role can't be on two channels; this is checked across all 10 channels, even when the expansion box isn't connected. Step timing (DIRSETUP, DIRHOLD, STEPLEN, STEPSPACE) and the home sequence are in each axis's [AXIS_x] / [JOINT_n] section of Rosetta.ini. Scale, Max Speed, Max Acceleration, backlash and the PID gains are this machine's own values, set in the Settings program and kept in /home/reuben/Documents/RosettaSet_v1.ini (Rosetta.ini's speed and acceleration figures are only the starting values for a new machine). See also, Mesa 7i92 Pin Mapping and Hardware Build ."},{"u":"DM542T.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"DM542T Digital Stepper Drive","h":[["Data-Needed-for-the-HAL-INI-Files","Data Needed for the HAL / INI Files"]],"x":"Data Needed for the HAL / INI Files Sequence chart of control signals Based on the chart to the right, the HAL configuration settings are: These are 5 μs = 5,000 ns DIRHOLD = 5000 DIRSETUP = 5000 These are 2.5 μs = 2,500 ns STEPLEN = 2500 STEPSPACE = 2500 These are the values in each [JOINT_n] / [SPINDLE_n] section of Rosetta.ini (DIRHOLD, DIRSETUP, STEPLEN, STEPSPACE). Note: Rosetta does not use a base thread - the Mesa 7i92 generates the step pulses in hardware, so the jitter and BASE_PERIOD calculation below is kept for reference only. Rosetta.ini has no BASE_PERIOD for the same reason. Max jitter was measured on the Raspberry Pi using the command, latency-test From that testing, the max jitter on the base thread was 16.4 μs. (See also, https://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/install/latency-test.html .) Using the data from above for the Stepper Online DM542T Drivers Stability before stepping = 5.0 μs Step length (2.5 μs x 2) = 5.0 μs Stability after stepping = 5.0 μs TOTAL = 15.0 μs Thusly, Base Period = 16.4 μs + 15.0 μs = 31.4 μs = 31,400 ns The value it would give (not used in Rosetta.ini) is: BASE_PERIOD = 31400 The full user manual is available."},{"u":"DataTypes.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Data Types","h":[["Key-Notes","Key Notes"]],"x":"Key Notes Both pins and signals have types, and signals can only be connected to pins of the same type. Currently there are 4 types: bit - a single TRUE/FALSE or ON/OFF value float - a 64 bit floating point value, with approximately 53 bits of resolution and over 1000 bits of dynamic range. u32 - a 32 bit unsigned integer, legal values are 0 to 4,294,967,295 s32 - a 32 bit signed integer, legal values are -2,147,483,647 to +2,147,483,647 Data types in Glade fields must match the ones used for pins or signals in the HAL. See also, https://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/hal/components.html#_signal_processing_realtime , especially for converting data types."},{"u":"DiagnosticTools.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Diagnostic Tools","h":[["Programs-supplied-with-LinuxCNC","Programs supplied with LinuxCNC"],["HALCMD","HALCMD"],["HAL-HISTOGRAM","HAL_HISTOGRAM"],["HALMETER","HALMETER"],["HALREPORT","HALREPORT"],["HALSCOPE","HALSCOPE"],["HALSHOW","HALSHOW"],["SIM-PIN","SIM_PIN"],["SIMULATE-PROBE","SIMULATE_PROBE"]],"x":"Programs supplied with LinuxCNC HALCMD halcmd is a command line tool for manipulating the HAL. Manual: http://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/hal/tools.html HAL_HISTOGRAM Hal-histogram is a command line utility to display histograms for HAL pins. Manual: http://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/hal/tools.html HALMETER Halmeter is used to display the current values of pins, signals, and parameters. It is used when LinuxCNC is running. Manual: http://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/hal/tools.html HALREPORT halreport is a command-line utility that generates a report about HAL connections for a running LinuxCNC (or other HAL) application. The report shows all signal connections and flags potential problems. Information included: System description and kernel version. Signals and all connected output, io, and input pins. Each pin’s component_function, thread, and addf-order. Non-realtime component pins having non-ordered functions. Identification of unknown functions for unhandled components. Signals with no output. Signals with no inputs. Functions with no addf. Warning tags for components marked as deprecated/obsolete in docs. Real names for pins that use alias names. The report can be generated from the command line and directed to an output file (or stdout if no outfilename is specified). Manual: http://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/hal/tools.html HALSCOPE Halscope is similar to Halmeter, but displays the values in an oscilloscope format (i.e., values over a time period). Manual: http://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/hal/tools.html HALSHOW Halshow is also similar to Halmeter, just a different way to see the values. Manual: http://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/hal/tools.html SIM_PIN Sim_pin is a command line utility to display and update any number of writable pins, parameters or signals. Manual: http://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/hal/tools.html SIMULATE_PROBE Simulate_probe is a a GUI used to simulate activation of the pin motion.probe-input. Manual: http://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/hal/tools.html"},{"u":"Diagnostics.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Diagnostics","h":[["Errors-in-LinuxCNC","Errors in LinuxCNC"]],"x":"Errors in LinuxCNC One of the best tools to use when trying to diagnose errors in LinuxCNC, especially for Glade, is to run the system in diagnostic mode by adding the “-d” option. Do not run Rosetta.ini directly. Rosetta is always started through the launch script, which first generates Rosetta.local.ini and the .local.hal files from this machine's settings (see Launching Rosetta ). Running Rosetta.ini directly would start LinuxCNC with the default axis layout, only the base box's card, and without the operator's limit switches, E-stop button, signal tower, lost-card E-stop, jog speeds, or units. To run in diagnostic mode, open a command prompt and enter this: cd /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta python3 Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py linuxcnc -d Rosetta.local.ini Errors encountered will be displayed in the command prompt area. LinuxCNC can also be run in verbose mode by adding the “-v” option."},{"u":"FieldTroubleshooting.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Field Troubleshooting","h":[["First-collect-this","First, collect this"],["LinuxCNC-won-t-start","LinuxCNC won't start"],["Base-box-lost-contact-or-Expansion-box-lost-contact","\"Base box lost contact\" or \"Expansion box lost contact\""],["Unexpected-realtime-delay","\"Unexpected realtime delay\""],["An-axis-doesn-t-move","An axis doesn't move"],["Limit-switch-E-stop-or-signal-tower-misbehaving","Limit switch, E-stop, or signal tower misbehaving"],["The-customer-s-tool-table-or-custom-HAL-changes-disappeared-after-an-update","The customer's tool table or custom HAL changes disappeared after an update"],["Going-back-to-the-previous-version","Going back to the previous version"]],"x":"First, collect this The software version: cd /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta git log -1 --oneline git status The generator's output (what LinuxCNC will load): cd /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta python3 Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py The network check (which cards answer, and the Raspberry Pi's wired address): bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Change_Network.sh check A copy of /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini . Whether the machine has an expansion box, and whether Expansion Box - Connected is ticked in the Settings program (Axis Selection tab). If LinuxCNC starts: the HAL state, from a terminal while it is running: halcmd show all > ~/all.txt Any error text from starting LinuxCNC in diagnostic mode (see Diagnostics ). The machine's serial number and version: the Rosetta O/S label on the side panel gives the version and date, and the serial number (if one was recorded) is in ~/.config/Rosetta/serial: cat ~/.config/Rosetta/serial tail -n 1 ~/.config/Rosetta/report_log.jsonl The last line of report_log.jsonl is the most recent version report (sent at each update). LinuxCNC won't start Run the generator by hand (above). If it reports a problem, it refuses to write the configuration. \"Reserved file name found - startup stopped\" - a file in the gcode folder starts with Rosetta or is named M100 - M199. Rename it - see Rosetta_Launch.sh . Check the Mesa cards are reachable: run the network check above, or ping 10.10.10.10 (base card) and ping 10.10.10.11 (expansion card), then read a card with mesaflash --device 7i92t --addr 10.10.10.10 --readhmid (see Mesa 7i92 ). The Raspberry Pi's wired address should be 10.10.10.100 ( ip -4 addr show eth0 ). If it isn't, run Rosetta_Change_Network.sh pi . A machine not yet moved to the 10.10.10.x network (Raspberry Pi on 192.168.1.120, card at its factory 192.168.1.121) still runs the base box, but the generator refuses to start with Expansion Box - Connected ticked. Move it with Rosetta_Change_Network.sh (see Installation and Setup for Debian 13 Trixie , Part 3), or untick the expansion box. Expansion Box - Connected is ticked but the expansion box is off or unplugged: switch it on and plug it into the switch in the base box, or untick it. A card that doesn't answer at its 10.10.10.x address: check its cable to the switch, its power, and that its W6 jumper is up (W5 down). Jumpers are read only at power-up. Nothing else may be plugged into the switch in the base box - no router or other computers. Look for hm2 / gpio / num_inms errors in the diagnostic output. The Raspberry Pi restarted by itself when the machine (or a box) was switched on: the Pi and the base card share one 5V supply - always switch the whole machine off and on together. Afterwards run Rosetta_Change_Network.sh check . \"Base box lost contact\" or \"Expansion box lost contact\" If either Mesa card stops answering while LinuxCNC runs (a network cable, the switch, or a box's power), LinuxCNC drops into E-stop, lights the signal tower's red light and sounds the fault siren (if used), and shows which box it was. (If it was the base box, the tower stays dark - its outputs are on the base card.) Check that box's network cable, the switch and its power, then press F1. While in E-stop, Rosetta tries to reconnect the card every few seconds; F1 resets the E-stop once the card answers again. \"Unexpected realtime delay\" LinuxCNC's 1 ms servo thread overran. With two cards each cycle waits for both, so there is less spare time than with one. Close the web browser and other busy programs while Rosetta runs - a busy browser can take two of the Raspberry Pi's four cores. Make sure nothing but the Raspberry Pi and the Mesa cards is on the switch. Watch the servo thread's worst time with halcmd show thread (the servo-thread Max Time, in ns). It should stay well under 1,000,000. If it keeps happening, the next step is reserving a processor core for LinuxCNC ( isolcpus ) - ask for support. An axis doesn't move Is it assigned to a channel, and is the motor on the plug for that channel? See Axis Mapping and the generator output. Is that channel on the expansion box (06 - 09)? Then Expansion Box - Connected must be ticked, or the role is left out. On an earlier 8-channel box, channels 06 and 07 now belong to the expansion box; move a role on them to a base channel (00 - 05). Is its ENA on (green) on the main panel, and the machine powered on (F2)? Is the DM542T's 5V/24V switch set to 5V ? At 24V (as new drivers come) the driver doesn't respond to the Mesa card. Is its Scale plausible? A value off by 1000x moves too little to see. Limit switch, E-stop, or signal tower misbehaving See Panel Accessories . Limit Jacks 4 - 6 (on the expansion box) only work while the expansion box is connected and ticked. The customer's tool table or custom HAL changes disappeared after an update Update Rosetta sets local changes to tracked files (such as Rosetta_Custom/Rosetta_Tool.tbl and Rosetta_Custom/Rosetta_Custom.hal) aside before pulling and puts them back afterwards. If the update changed the same lines, or the machine was updated before 30 September 2026 (when the update didn't put them back), they are still in the stash - see Updating Rosetta . Going back to the previous version See Updating Rosetta . A machine already moved to the 10.10.10.x network needs its network put back before it runs a version from before the two-box design."},{"u":"FileLocations.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"File Locations","h":[["Per-machine-files-not-in-the-repository","Per-machine files (not in the repository)"],["home-reuben-linuxcnc-configs-Rosetta","/home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta"],["Rosetta-Custom","Rosetta_Custom/"],["Rosetta-Display","Rosetta_Display/"],["Rosetta-Setup","Rosetta_Setup/"],["Rosetta-Subroutines","Rosetta_Subroutines/"],["Other-files-in-the-top-folder","Other files in the top folder"],["docs","docs/"]],"x":"All Rosetta files are kept in the Git repository on GitHub (web site), and installed at /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta Update Rosetta ( Rosetta_Update.sh ) pulls the latest versions of these files from GitHub - see Updating Rosetta . The only per-machine settings file lives outside the repository, in /home/reuben/Documents. Per-machine files (not in the repository) File Purpose /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini This machine's settings: axis/channel assignments (channels 00 - 09), whether the expansion box is connected, scale, Max Speed, Max Acceleration, stepgen limits, PID gains and PID Max Output, backlash, jog speeds, measurement system, device names, limit jacks and switch type, E-stop button, signal tower, sounds, and the main panel's remembered values. Written by the Settings program, by Rosetta_Scale_Persist.py at LinuxCNC shutdown, and (new settings only) by an update. JSON with comment lines, despite the .ini name - don't edit it by hand. Back this up . /home/reuben/Documents/ *.ini (other than RosettaSet_v1.ini) Copies made with Save All Settings As..., and Save Subset exports. Back Up to USB copies them too. /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini.bak- date-time The settings file as it was before an update added new settings. /home/reuben/ .axisrc AXIS window size/position; copied from Rosetta_Setup/axisrc at install. /home/reuben/ClamAv/ Virus scan log and quarantined files. /home/reuben/.config/Rosetta/ serial This machine's serial number, if one was given (Rosetta_Set_Serial_Number.sh). /home/reuben/.config/Rosetta/ report_log.jsonl A copy of every version report sent by an update (see Rosetta_Update.sh ). /home/reuben/.config/Rosetta/ shortcuts-added Marker: the folder shortcuts have been added (Rosetta_Add_Shortcuts.sh). /home/reuben/ Rosetta_Update.sh An old copy left by the installer. Not used - ignore it. /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/ Rosetta.local.ini , Rosetta.local.hal , Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.local.hal , Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Splash.local.ngc Generated at every launch from Rosetta.ini / Rosetta.hal / Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.hal and RosettaSet_v1.ini - these are what LinuxCNC actually loads. Never edit them - they are overwritten at the next start. See Launching Rosetta . /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/ gcode/ Operator's G-code programs (not in the repository). AXIS's Open dialog starts here. No file here may start with Rosetta or be named M100 - M199 - startup stops if one does (see Rosetta_Launch.sh ). /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/ sim.var LinuxCNC's parameter file (written while it runs). /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta File Purpose Edit? Rosetta_Launch.sh What the desktop icon runs: generates the .local files, then starts LinuxCNC on Rosetta.local.ini. No Rosetta_Settings.sh Starts the Rosetta Settings program. No Rosetta_Change_Network.sh Sets up the Mesa cards' addresses (base 10.10.10.10, expansion 10.10.10.11) and the Raspberry Pi's wired connection (10.10.10.100); check shows which cards answer. See Mesa 7i92 . No Rosetta_Backup.sh What the Back Up to USB icon runs (Rosetta_Display/Rosetta_Backup.py) - see Rosetta_Backup.sh . No Rosetta_Update.sh What the Update icon runs: pulls updates from GitHub, adds new settings, runs one-time steps, sends the version report, then runs apt update / upgrade - see Rosetta_Update.sh . No Rosetta.ini Master LinuxCNC configuration template: step timing, home sequence, and the starting speed and acceleration limits for a new machine. This machine's own tuning is in RosettaSet_v1.ini. No Rosetta.hal Realtime HAL template: Mesa card(s), stepgens, PID loops, E-stop latch. Its loadrt hm2_eth line is rewritten at every launch for one or both cards. No Rosetta_Shutdown.hal Runs Rosetta_Scale_Persist.py at shutdown to save live values. No RosettaStartupImage.gif Splash image shown while LinuxCNC starts. No .axisrc Copy of the AXIS window settings. No Rosetta_Custom/ File Purpose Edit? Rosetta_Custom.hal The place for machine-specific HAL additions (loaded after Rosetta.hal). Yes* Rosetta_Tool.tbl LinuxCNC tool table. Yes* * These are tracked in Git, so Update Rosetta stashes local changes to them - see Updating Rosetta . Rosetta_Display/ File Purpose Rosetta_main.py Python handler for the side panel and the Help / License tabs (see GladeVCP ). Rosetta_Panel_v1.ui The main side panel (Indexing, Sync Move, Threading). Rosetta_Tab_Help_v1.ui , Rosetta_Tab_TsCs_v1.ui The ℛ Help and ℛ License tabs. Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.hal HAL wiring for the panel's ENA buttons and status (template for the .local copy). Rosetta_Settings.py , Rosetta_Settings_v1.ui The standalone Rosetta Settings program. rosetta_settings_io.py Shared reader/writer for RosettaSet_v1.ini. Rosetta_Settings_Restore.py Applies saved scale / PID / velocity values to the HAL pins at startup. Rosetta_Scale_Persist.py Saves the live values back to RosettaSet_v1.ini at shutdown. Rosetta_Splash.ngc The empty start-up G-code file, used when X and Z aren't both assigned (otherwise the launch writes Rosetta_Splash.local.ngc). Rosetta_Backup.py The Back Up to USB program. Rosetta_Program_Estimate.py Works out the progress bar's percentages when a G-code program starts. Sounds/ Rosetta_Done.wav, Rosetta_Pause.wav and Rosetta_Fault.wav - the done and pause sounds and the fault siren; Rosetta_Abort.wav - played when startup is stopped by a reserved file name. Images/ Panel images. Rosetta_Setup/ File Purpose Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py Builds Rosetta.local.ini and the .local.hal files at each launch (see Launching Rosetta ). Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh Installer for new systems ( Installation and Setup for Debian 13 Trixie ); sets up the base card with Rosetta_Change_Network.sh. Rosetta_Update_Settings.py Run by Rosetta_Update.sh: adds new settings to this machine's RosettaSet_v1.ini (see Updating Rosetta ). Rosetta_Add_Shortcuts.sh Adds the Rosetta and Gcode folder shortcuts, once. Run by the installer and by updates. Rosetta_Set_Serial_Number.sh Asks for this machine's serial number. Run by the installer, and by updates until one is given. Rosetta_Report_Version.py Sends the version report at each update. REB_Install_Debian_12_Bookworm.sh The old installer for Debian 12 (Bookworm). No longer maintained - not used for new machines. RosettaSet_v1.ini Starting settings copied to /home/reuben/Documents at install. axisrc Copied to /home/reuben/.axisrc at install. MesaCard/ 7i92 firmware (7i92t_Rosetta.bin, used on both the base and expansion cards), pin file, and VHDL source (PIN_Rosetta_34.vhd). Images/ Desktop and menu icons (including the Gcode folder's Rosetta_Folder-Gcode.png), wallpaper, and logo. Rosetta_Subroutines/ File Purpose rosetta_thread.ngc , rosetta_thread_return.ngc G-code subroutines used by the Threading tab. Other files in the top folder LICENSE - the MIT/X Consortium License (see Licensing ). README.md - a short description shown on GitHub. docs/ Design notes for developers (e.g., settings_file.md describes RosettaSet_v1.ini in detail), and Rosetta_Tracker.md - the list of open, untested and recently settled items."},{"u":"FontsUsed.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Fonts Used","h":[],"x":"The fonts used for the logo on front of case: Libre Baskerville → Rosetta The image was developed using https://Lucid.app ."},{"u":"GladeVCP.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Glade Virtual Control Panel","h":[["Key-Notes","Key Notes"],["The-Settings-program-is-not-GladeVCP","The Settings program is not GladeVCP"],["Data-types","Data types"],["Programming-Manual","Programming Manual"]],"x":"Key Notes Rosetta's screens are built with GladeVCP. All the files are in /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Display. File Purpose Rosetta_Panel_v1.ui The main side panel inside AXIS: each axis's ENA, Feed Rate and Device, the Indexing / Sync Move / Threading tabs, and the progress bar. Loaded by Rosetta.ini's [DISPLAY] GLADEVCP line; AXIS starts it as its own gladevcp process and shows it inside its window. Rosetta_Tab_Help_v1.ui Rosetta_Tab_TsCs_v1.ui The ℛ Help and ℛ License tabs. Each is started by its own EMBED_TAB_COMMAND in Rosetta.ini, so each also runs as a separate process . Rosetta_main.py The one Python handler for all three panels above (each process runs its own copy). Because they are separate processes, they can only share information through HAL pins or RosettaSet_v1.ini. Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.hal Loaded after the GUI starts: wires the panel's ENA buttons and indicator pins into HAL. (LinuxCNC actually loads the generated Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.local.hal copy - see Launching Rosetta .) The Settings program is not GladeVCP The Rosetta Settings program ( Rosetta_Settings.py with Rosetta_Settings_v1.ui ) is a standalone GTK program, run from the Applications menu or Rosetta_Settings.sh . It only runs while LinuxCNC is not running, and writes its settings to /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini , which the next launch reads. See Rosetta_Settings.sh . Data types Widget and HAL pin data types must match - see Data Types . Programming Manual See, https://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/gui/gladevcp.html ."},{"u":"HALFile.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"HAL File","h":[["HAL-Commands","HAL Commands"],["addf","addf"],["loadrt","loadrt"],["loadusr","loadusr"],["net","net"],["setp","setp"],["sets","sets"],["unlinkp","unlinkp"]],"x":"Pins & Signals halmeter is a good way to observe the pins & signals. HAL Commands See also, LinuxCNC HAL Basics Manual , & LinuxCNC Core Components Manual . addf The addf command adds a function to a real-time thread. If the StepConf wizard was used to create the configuration, two threads have been created (“base-thread” and “servo-thread”). addf adds function functname to thread threadname. Default is to add the function in the order they are in the file. If position is specified, adds the function to that spot in the thread. A negative position indicates the position with respect to the end of the thread. For example 1 is start of thread, -1 is the end of the thread, -3 is third from the end. loadrt The command loadrt loads a real time HAL component. Real time component functions need to be added to a thread to be updated at the rate of the thread. You cannot load a non-realtime component into the realtime space. Syntax and Example loadrt <component> <options> loadrt mux4 count=1 loadrt components list: https://linuxcnc.org/docs/html/hal/components.html loadusr The command loadusr loads a non-realtime HAL component. Non-realtime programs are their own separate processes, which optionally talk to other HAL components via pins and parameters. You cannot load realtime components into non-realtime space. net The command net creates a connection between a signal and one or more pins. If the signal does not exist net creates the new signal. This replaces the need to use the command newsig. The optional direction arrows <=, => and <=> make it easier to follow the logic when reading a net command line and are not used by the net command. The direction arrows must be separated by a space from the pin names. Syntax and Example net signal-name pin-name <optional arrow> <optional second pin-name> net home-x joint.0.home-sw-in <= parport.0.pin-11-in In the above example home-x is the signal name, joint.0.home-sw-in is a Direction IN pin, <= is the optional direction arrow, and parport.0.pin-11-in is a Direction OUT pin. This may seem confusing but the in and out labels for a parallel port pin indicates the physical way the pin works not how it is handled in HAL. A pin can be connected to a signal if it obeys the following rules An IN pin can always be connected to a signal. An IO pin can be connected unless there’s an OUT pin on the signal. An OUT pin can be connected only if there are no other OUT or IO pins on the signal. The same signal-name can be used in multiple net commands to connect additional pins, as long as the rules above are obeyed. setp The command setp sets the value of a pin or parameter. The valid values will depend on the type of the pin or parameter. It is an error if the data types do not match. Some components have parameters that need to be set before use. Parameters can be set before use or while running as needed. You cannot use setp on a pin that is connected to a signal. Syntax and Example setp <pin/parameter-name> <value> setp parport.0.pin-08-out TRUE sets The command sets sets the value of a signal. Syntax and Example sets <signal-name> <value> net mysignal and2.0.in0 pyvcp.my-led sets mysignal 1 It is an error if: The signal-name does not exist If the signal already has a writer If value is not the correct type for the signal unlinkp The command unlinkp unlinks a pin from the connected signal. If no signal was connected to the pin prior running the command, nothing happens. The unlinkp command is useful for troubleshooting. Syntax and Example unlinkp <pin-name> unlinkp parport.0.pin-02-out"},{"u":"HALPrograms.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"HAL Support Programs","h":[["Key-Notes","Key Notes"],["halcmd","halcmd"],["halmeter","halmeter"],["halscope","halscope"],["halrun","halrun"],["halshow","halshow"],["halcmd-show-thread","halcmd show thread"]],"x":"Key Notes halcmd Some key commands to execute are: halcmd show all > all.txt halcmd show signal | grep spindle.1 halcmd -kf More detailed information can be found in the man(ual) page for halcmd. Run man halcmd in a terminal window. halmeter Used to show values of signals and pins. halscope Used to show values of signals and pins in an oscilloscope format. halrun Starts a HAL session without LinuxCNC (or runs a HAL file). Not normally needed on Rosetta - use halcmd while LinuxCNC is running. halshow A window listing every pin, signal and parameter, with a watch list. Start it from AXIS (Machine > Show HAL Configuration) or with halshow in a terminal. halcmd show thread Shows each realtime thread's time. The servo-thread Max Time (in ns) should stay well under 1,000,000 (1 ms) - see Field Troubleshooting ."},{"u":"HardwareBuild.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Hardware Build for the Rosetta","h":[],"x":"Building a Rosetta: 1. Parts · 2. Common build steps · 3a. With the Rosetta PCB · 3b. Without the PCB · 4. Software A Rosetta is a base box and, optionally, an expansion box . There are two ways to build the boxes: With the Rosetta PCB - the Rosetta circuit board connects everything, and has the signal tower's relays built in. Without the PCB - the Mesa card's headers are brought out to screw-terminal breakout boards, and everything is wired to those. Both give the same machine, and run the same software. Step Page 1 Hardware Parts - one parts list for both builds, marked with which build each part is for 2 Hardware Build Common Steps - the two boxes, network, power, DM542T settings, channel plugs and Mesa cards 3a Hardware Build With Rosetta PCB 3b Hardware Build Without PCB 4 Software Installation and Setup - the same for both builds"},{"u":"HardwareBuildCommonSteps.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Hardware Build - Common Steps","h":[["Connections_","Connections"],["The-Wired-Network","The Wired Network"],["Power","Power"],["Development-8-channel-Boxes","Development (8-channel) Boxes"],["Configure-the-Parts","Configure the Parts"],["1-1-DM542T-Drivers","1.1 DM542T Drivers"],["1-2-Channel-Plugs","1.2 Channel Plugs"],["1-3-Mesa-7i92TH-Cards","1.3 Mesa 7i92TH Cards"]],"x":"Building a Rosetta: 1. Parts · 2. Common build steps · 3a. With the Rosetta PCB · 3b. Without the PCB · 4. Software These steps are the same whether the boxes are built with the Rosetta circuit board or with breakout boards. Get the parts first ( Hardware Parts ), do these steps, then continue with the page for your build. The two boxes The base box holds the Raspberry Pi, an Ethernet switch, one Mesa 7i92TH card and 6 stepper motor drivers (channels 00 - 05). Limit Jacks 1 - 3, the E-stop button and the signal tower are on the base box. The expansion box is optional. It holds a second Mesa 7i92TH card and 4 more stepper motor drivers (channels 06 - 09), plus Limit Jacks 4 - 6. It connects to the switch in the base box with one Ethernet cable. With both boxes, there is one channel for each of the 10 axes and spindles (Sp0, Sp1, X, Z, U, V, W, A, B and C). Connections The overall connections for this system are as shown to the right. The items in the blue box are shipped as the Rosetta system. The boxes in gray and the associated cabling are optional. Overall Connections The Wired Network The Raspberry Pi and the Mesa cards talk to each other over a private wired network, through the Ethernet switch in the base box. Plug the Raspberry Pi, the base card and (if used) the expansion card into the switch. Nothing else goes on the switch - no router, no internet connection, no other computers. Other traffic on that network upsets LinuxCNC's timing. The Raspberry Pi reaches the internet over WiFi instead. The addresses used on this network are: Raspberry Pi 10.10.10.100 Base card 10.10.10.10 Expansion card 10.10.10.11 The software setup sets these addresses. Until then, a new Mesa card answers at its factory address, 192.168.1.121. Power In the base box, one 24 VDC to 5 VDC power module powers both the Raspberry Pi and the base card. Switching the card on while the Raspberry Pi is running restarts the Raspberry Pi without shutting it down, which can damage its microSD card. Always switch the whole machine off and on together. Development (8-channel) Boxes 100 Mbit Ethernet Crossover Cable The earlier 8-channel boxes are used as a base box: channels 00 - 05 work, and their other two plugs are not used. Some of them have no Ethernet switch: the Raspberry Pi is wired straight to the single Mesa card with an Ethernet crossover cable. An expansion box can't be added to such a box until a switch is fitted. The wiring configuration for a 100 Mbit Ethernet crossover cable is shown to the right. Pins 4, 5, 7, & 8 still need to be wired to make this cable work as expected. There is no benefit to using a gigabit crossover cable. Configure the Parts 1.1 DM542T Drivers DM542T Driver Configure each DM542T driver (6 in the base box, 4 in the expansion box) Set the voltage on the 5V/24V switch to 5V (that switch is located on the side of the DM542T, not the top where the DIP switches are). New drivers come set to 24V. At 24V the driver does not respond to the Mesa card's signals, and that motor will not move. Set the DIP switches as shown in the table below, using the values in the highlighted rows. Peak Current RMS Current SW1 SW2 SW3 Default Setting Axes Motor 1.00A 0.71A ON ON ON 1.46A 1.04A OFF ON ON X, Z, U, V, & W NEMA 17 1.91A 1.36A ON OFF ON 2.37A 1.69A OFF OFF ON 2.84A 2.03A ON ON OFF Sp0, Sp1, A, B, & C NEMA 23 3.31A 2.36A OFF ON OFF 3.76A 2.69A ON OFF OFF 4.50A 3.20A OFF OFF OFF Idle Current % SW4 90% ON 50% OFF Micro-steps Steps/rev on 1.8° motor SW5 SW6 SW7 SW8 16 3,200 ON ON OFF ON 32 6,400 OFF ON OFF ON 64 12,800 ON OFF OFF ON 128 25,600 OFF OFF OFF ON 1.2 Channel Plugs The channel plugs are laid out with the even numbers on the top row and the odd numbers on the bottom row. Wire each plug to the step generator with the same number, so the labels on the box match the software. Base box Top row: 00 / 02 / 04 Bottom row: 01 / 03 / 05 Expansion box Top row: 06 / 08 Bottom row: 07 / 09 The axis or spindle on each channel is chosen in the Rosetta Settings program, so any of them can go on any channel. The starting settings are: Base box 00 X, 01 Z, 02 B, 03 W, 04 Sp0, 05 Sp1 Expansion box 06 U, 07 V, 08 A, 09 C 1.3 Mesa 7i92TH Cards W4 jumper location W6 jumper location Leave the W4 jumper at its factory setting ( down - P2's breakout power off). See note 3 on Mesa 7i92TH Pin Mapping . Leave the W5 and W6 jumpers at their factory setting (both down ). The card then answers at its factory address, 192.168.1.121. Label each card Base or Expansion before it goes into its box. The two cards are set up the same way, but are given different addresses. The firmware, the address and the jumper change are done during the software setup , which tells you when to move the jumpers. Next: building the boxes - 3a. With the Rosetta PCB or 3b. Without the PCB ."},{"u":"HardwareBuildWithRosettaPCB.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Hardware Build - With the Rosetta PCB","h":[["Network-Connections","Network Connections"],["One-board-two-boxes","One board, two boxes"],["Assembling-the-PCB","Assembling the PCB"],["Base-Box","Base Box"],["Mounting-the-PCB","Mounting the PCB"],["Mesa-card-to-PCB","Mesa card to PCB"],["PCB-to-the-DM542T-drivers","PCB to the DM542T drivers"],["Limit-jacks-E-stop-button-and-signal-tower","Limit jacks, E-stop button and signal tower"],["Expansion-Box","Expansion Box"],["Mounting-the-PCB_","Mounting the PCB"],["Mesa-card-to-PCB_","Mesa card to PCB"],["PCB-to-the-DM542T-drivers_","PCB to the DM542T drivers"],["Limit-jacks","Limit jacks"],["Which-pin-does-what","Which pin does what"]],"x":"Building a Rosetta: 1. Parts · 2. Common build steps · 3a. With the Rosetta PCB · 3b. Without the PCB · 4. Software This page is for building the boxes with the Rosetta circuit board. The board connects the Mesa 7i92TH card to the DM542T drivers, the limit jacks, the E-stop button and the signal tower, and has the signal tower's relays built in. This page is an outline. It will be completed when the PCB design is final. Before starting: Get the parts marked Both and Rosetta PCB on Hardware Parts . Do the common build steps (DM542T switches, Mesa jumpers, network and power). Network Connections One board, two boxes Both boxes use the same PCB . The expansion box's board has fewer parts fitted: Part of the PCB Base box Expansion box Driver connections 6 (channels 00 - 05) 4 (channels 06 - 09) Limit jack connections 3 (Limit Jacks 1 - 3) 3 (Limit Jacks 4 - 6) E-stop button connection Fitted Not fitted Signal tower relays Fitted Not fitted Mesa card headers used P1 and P2 P2 only Other parts To be completed To be completed Assembling the PCB To be completed - which parts are fitted for each box, and in what order. Base Box Mounting the PCB To be completed Mesa card to PCB Two IDC26 ribbon cables (part 1401), from the card's P2 and P1 headers to the PCB. To be completed - which PCB connector each goes to, and the red stripe (pin 1) orientation. PCB to the DM542T drivers Two IDC6 ribbon cables for each driver (part 1402). To be completed - which PCB connector is which channel, and how each cable connects at the driver. Limit jacks, E-stop button and signal tower To be completed - where the jacks connect to the PCB. The signal tower's relays are on the PCB, so the tower's jack connects to it directly; see Signal Tower Relays for what the relays switch. Expansion Box Mounting the PCB To be completed Mesa card to PCB One IDC26 ribbon cable, from the expansion card's P2 header to the PCB (its P1 isn't used). To be completed - which PCB connector, and the red stripe orientation. PCB to the DM542T drivers Two IDC6 ribbon cables for each of the 4 drivers (part 2402). To be completed Limit jacks To be completed Which pin does what The PCB carries each signal to the same Mesa card pin as a breakout-board build, so the software doesn't need to know which build a machine is. The full map is on Mesa 7i92TH Pin Mapping . Next: Software Installation and Setup"},{"u":"HardwareBuildWithoutPCB.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Hardware Build - Without the Rosetta PCB","h":[["Power-Connections","Power Connections"],["Network-Connections","Network Connections"],["Base-Box","Base Box"],["Stepper-motor-drivers-channels-00-05","Stepper motor drivers (channels 00 - 05)"],["Limit-jacks-E-stop-button-and-signal-tower","Limit jacks, E-stop button and signal tower"],["Other-terminals","Other terminals"],["Expansion-Box","Expansion Box"],["Stepper-motor-drivers-channels-06-09","Stepper motor drivers (channels 06 - 09)"],["Limit-jacks","Limit jacks"],["Other-terminals_","Other terminals"],["Connecting-a-DM542T-driver","Connecting a DM542T driver"],["Limit-switches-and-E-stop-button","Limit switches and E-stop button"],["Signal-tower","Signal tower"]],"x":"Building a Rosetta: 1. Parts · 2. Common build steps · 3a. With the Rosetta PCB · 3b. Without the PCB · 4. Software This page is for building the boxes without the Rosetta circuit board. Each Mesa 7i92TH card's 26-pin headers are brought out to IDC26 breakout boards (26 screw terminals on a DIN rail) with IDC26 ribbon cables, and everything else is wired to those terminals. The software is exactly the same as for a machine built with the Rosetta PCB: it only knows the card's pins, so the wiring below must match it. Before starting: Get the parts marked Both and Without PCB on Hardware Parts . Do the common build steps (DM542T switches, Mesa jumpers, network and power). Terminal numbers below are the card's 26-pin header pin numbers, which match the breakout board's terminal numbers when the ribbon cable is fitted the right way round (the red stripe on pin 1 at both ends). Mesa's pin file (7i92t_Rosetta.pin) numbers the same pins differently, as a 25-pin plug would - don't wire from it. The full map is on Mesa 7i92TH Pin Mapping . Power Connections Network Connections Base Box Two breakout boards: one on the card's P2 header and one on P1 . Stepper motor drivers (channels 00 - 05) Channel Breakout Board Breakout Board Terminal Step to PUL+ Dir to DIR+ Enable to ENA+ Common to PUL- DIR- ENA- 00 P2 1 2 4 10 01 P2 5 6 8 10 02 P2 9 11 15 10 03 P2 17 19 23 10 04 P1 1 2 4 10 05 P1 5 6 8 10 Limit jacks, E-stop button and signal tower Function Breakout Board Breakout Board Terminal Other wire to Limit Jack 1 P2 3 GND terminal Limit Jack 2 P2 7 GND terminal Limit Jack 3 P2 13 GND terminal E-stop button P1 21 GND terminal Signal tower - Relay Module 1 (red light) P2 25 Relay Module 1 - IN Signal tower - Relay Module 2 (green light) P1 25 Relay Module 2 - IN Signal tower - Relay Module 3 (siren) P1 13 Relay Module 3 - IN Other terminals GND: P2 terminals 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24; P1 terminals 10, 12, 14, 16. 5 V: P1 terminals 18, 20, 22, 24 (with the card's W3 jumper up). Never connect these to a GND terminal or to the 24 V supply. Whether they can power the three relay modules is To be confirmed . Leave unconnected: P1 terminals 9, 11, 15, 17, 19, 23 (they drove channels 06 and 07 on the earlier 8-channel boxes) and terminal 26 on each header. Spare inputs: P2 terminal 21; P1 terminals 3, 7. Expansion Box One breakout board, on the expansion card's P2 header. Its P1 header isn't used. Stepper motor drivers (channels 06 - 09) Channel Breakout Board Breakout Board Terminal Step to PUL+ Dir to DIR+ Enable to ENA+ Common to PUL- DIR- ENA- 06 P2 1 2 4 10 07 P2 5 6 8 10 08 P2 9 11 15 10 09 P2 17 19 23 10 Limit jacks Function Breakout Board Breakout Board Terminal Other wire to Limit Jack 4 P2 3 GND terminal Limit Jack 5 P2 7 GND terminal Limit Jack 6 P2 13 GND terminal Other terminals GND: P2 terminals 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24. Leave unconnected: terminal 25 and terminal 26. Spare input: terminal 21. Connecting a DM542T driver Each driver has three signal pairs. Set its 5V/24V switch to 5V first (see common steps ). DM542T terminal Connect to PUL+ the channel's Step terminal DIR+ the channel's Dir terminal ENA+ the channel's Enable terminal PUL- , DIR- , ENA- joined together, then to a GND terminal on the same breakout board - terminal 10 (the Common column above). All the drivers on one breakout board can share that terminal. On the P1 board, terminals 18, 20, 22 and 24 are 5 V, not GND - never connect the drivers' - terminals to them. Limit switches and E-stop button Wire a normally closed switch between its terminal and a GND terminal. The card's pull-up makes an open switch (tripped, broken or unplugged) read as tripped. No other parts are needed. Limit switch jacks are GX-12/2, the E-stop button jack is GX-16/2. See the User Manual's Limit Switch Cabling and E-Stop Button Cabling (User Manual). Signal tower A breakout board has no relays, so three separate single-relay modules (part 1260) switch the tower: Relay Module 1 the red light, Relay Module 2 the green light and Relay Module 3 the siren. Signal Tower Relay Connections Each module is wired the same way: Relay module terminal Connect to VCC +5 VDC IN its breakout board terminal (table above): Relay Module 1 - P2 terminal 25; Relay Module 2 - P1 terminal 25; Relay Module 3 - P1 terminal 13 GND a Mesa GND terminal on a breakout board - P1 terminal 12 (all three modules can share it) COM its lamp or the siren NO +24 VDC from the power supply (all three modules) NC not connected Two separate grounds: the modules' 5 V side (VCC, IN, GND) uses the Mesa card's ground, as above. The tower's 24 V side does not: the tower's common wire goes to the GND of the 24 VDC power supply , never to a breakout board terminal. Each module must switch on when its input is pulled low (a \"low-level trigger\" module). The card holds its pins high whenever LinuxCNC isn't running, so a high-level module would turn the tower on at start-up and after shut-down. See also Signal Tower Relays . Next: Software Installation and Setup"},{"u":"HardwareParts.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Hardware Parts for the Rosetta","h":[],"x":"Building a Rosetta: 1. Parts · 2. Common build steps · 3a. With the Rosetta PCB · 3b. Without the PCB · 4. Software This is the one parts list for both ways of building a Rosetta. The Applies to column says which build each part is for: Both Needed for either build. Rosetta PCB Only when building with the Rosetta circuit board - see Hardware Build With Rosetta PCB . Without PCB Only when building without it, using breakout boards - see Hardware Build Without PCB . Without the PCB, the case and power parts (1000 - 1199 and 2000 - 2199) are the builder's own choice, so they are listed for the Rosetta PCB build only. The expansion box (part numbers 2000 - 2499) is optional. Part numbers stay the same whichever build is chosen. # Item Qty Source Source P/N Applies to Comments Base Box 1000 Case 1001 Case 1 Rosetta PCB 1100 Power 1101 Power Switch 1 Astrodyne TDI 082M.01001.00-LS Rosetta PCB 1102 Power Cord 1 Schurter 6009.1214 Rosetta PCB 1102 Power Cord 1 Without PCB Use the appropriate power cord for where you will be powering this. 1103 Power Outfeed Jack 1 Schurter 6600.4315 Rosetta PCB 1111 24 VDC Power Supply 1 MeanWell LRS 100-24 Both 1112 24 VDC to 5 VDC power module 1 Both Powers both the Raspberry Pi and the Mesa card. 1121 110 VAC power cable, #14 1 Both 1122 24 VDC power cable, #26 Both 1200 Electronics Inside the Box 1201 Raspberry Pi 5 8 GB RAM 128 GB microSD card 1 CanaKit PI5-8GB-STR128-C4-BLK Both 1210 Mesa 7i92TH Anything I/O Ethernet card 1 Mesa US 7i92TH Both The base card. Its address is 10.10.10.10. 1220 Stepper Motor Drivers 6 Stepper Online DM542T Both Channels 00 - 05. be sure to use high quality drivers; the ones from Stepper Online are recommended. 1230 Rosetta PCB 1 Rosetta PCB The same PCB is used in both boxes; the expansion box's has fewer parts fitted. 1240 Ethernet switch, gigabit, unmanaged 1 Both Only the Raspberry Pi and the Mesa cards are connected to it. 1250 IDC26 breakout board (26 screw terminals, DIN rail) 2 Jienk (Amazon) B0CBV9NDJY Without PCB One for each of the base card's headers, P1 and P2. See Hardware Build Without PCB . 1260 Relay module, 1 channel, 5 VDC coil, low-level trigger 3 Without PCB Relay Modules 1, 2 and 3: one each for the red light, the green light and the siren - only if a signal tower is used. Each must switch on when its input is pulled low. The Rosetta PCB has these relays built in. See Signal Tower Relays . 1300 Panel Mount Parts 1301 GX-16/4 stepper motor jacks 6 Both Channels 00 - 05 1302 GX-12/2 limit switch jacks 3 Both Limit Jacks 1 - 3 1303 GX-16/2 E-stop button jack 1 Both Optional E-stop button 1304 GX-12/4 signal tower jack 1 Both Optional signal tower 1400 Cables within the Box 1401 IDC26-IDC26 Ribbon Cable 2 Both 1402 IDC6-IDC6 Ribbon Cable 12 Rosetta PCB 2 for each stepper motor driver 1403 Ethernet cable (CAT-6 minimum, CAT-8 recommended) 2 Both Raspberry Pi to switch, and switch to the base card 1404 Hookup wire, 22 AWG, stranded Without PCB From the breakout boards to the DM542T drivers, jacks and relay module. Expansion Box (optional) 2000 Case 2001 Case 1 Rosetta PCB 2100 Power 2101 Power parts: a 24 VDC supply for the 4 drivers, and a 5 VDC supply for the expansion card To be confirmed Rosetta PCB 2200 Electronics Inside the Box 2210 Mesa 7i92TH Anything I/O Ethernet card 1 Mesa US 7i92TH Both The expansion card. Its address is 10.10.10.11. 2220 Stepper Motor Drivers 4 Stepper Online DM542T Both Channels 06 - 09 2230 Rosetta PCB 1 Rosetta PCB The same PCB as the base box (1230), with fewer parts fitted - see Hardware Build With Rosetta PCB . 2250 IDC26 breakout board (26 screw terminals, DIN rail) 1 Jienk (Amazon) B0CBV9NDJY Without PCB For the expansion card's P2 (its P1 isn't used). 2300 Panel Mount Parts 2301 GX-16/4 stepper motor jacks 4 Both Channels 06 - 09 2302 GX-12/2 limit switch jacks 3 Both Limit Jacks 4 - 6 2400 Cables within the Box 2401 IDC26-IDC26 Ribbon Cable 1 Both 2402 IDC6-IDC6 Ribbon Cable 8 Rosetta PCB 2 for each stepper motor driver 2403 Ethernet cable (CAT-6 minimum, CAT-8 recommended) 1 Both Expansion card to the switch in the base box 2404 Hookup wire, 22 AWG, stranded Without PCB From the breakout board to the DM542T drivers and jacks. Other 1900 Other 1901 Computer Monitor (1920x1080) 1 LG 24MR400-B.AUSQ Both 1902 Keyboard & Mouse 1 Kensington K72436AM Both Next: 2. Common build steps"},{"u":"InstallationAndCheckoutList.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Installation and Checkout List","h":[],"x":"Print this and complete it for each system: Installation and Checkout List ."},{"u":"InstallationAndSetupForDebian13Trixie.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Software Installation and Setup for Debian 13 Trixie","h":[["Prerequisites","Prerequisites"],["Part-1-Build-the-Boot-Drive-for-LinuxCNC","Part 1 - Build the Boot Drive for LinuxCNC"],["1-1-Get-the-Raspberry-Pi-Imager","1.1 Get the Raspberry Pi Imager"],["1-2-Download-the-LinuxCNC-Image-File","1.2 Download the LinuxCNC Image File"],["1-3-Create-the-Boot-Disk-for-the-Raspberry-Pi","1.3 Create the Boot Disk for the Raspberry Pi"],["Part-2-Configure-Linux-on-the-Raspberry-Pi","Part 2 - Configure Linux on the Raspberry Pi"],["2-1-Start-the-Raspberry-Pi","2.1 Start the Raspberry Pi"],["2-2-Set-System-Defaults","2.2 Set System Defaults"],["2-3-Set-Wired-Network-Connection-Test-Network-Connections","2.3 Set Wired Network Connection & Test Network Connections"],["2-4-Set-Rest-of-System-Options","2.4 Set Rest of System Options"],["2-5-Download-the-Files-Needed","2.5 Download the Files Needed"],["2-6-Clean-Up-the-Desktop","2.6 Clean Up the Desktop"],["Part-3-Setup-LinuxCNC","Part 3 - Setup LinuxCNC"],["3-1-Run-the-Automated-Install-Base-Box","3.1 Run the Automated Install (Base Box)"],["3-1-1-Move-the-Base-Card-s-Jumpers","3.1.1 Move the Base Card's Jumpers"],["3-2-Add-the-Expansion-Box","3.2 Add the Expansion Box"],["3-3-If-a-Script-Fails-Doing-It-by-Hand","3.3 If a Script Fails - Doing It by Hand"],["3-3-1-Set-Up-a-Mesa-Card-by-Hand","3.3.1 Set Up a Mesa Card by Hand"],["3-3-2-Undo-Put-a-Card-or-the-Raspberry-Pi-Back","3.3.2 Undo: Put a Card or the Raspberry Pi Back"],["3-3-3-Automated-Install-Programs","3.3.3 Automated Install Programs"],["3-4-Schedule-the-Virus-Scan","3.4 Schedule the Virus Scan"],["3-5-Set-the-Wallpaper","3.5 Set the Wallpaper"],["3-6-Set-the-Icons-on-the-Desktop","3.6 Set the Icons on the Desktop"],["3-6-1-Rosetta","3.6.1 Rosetta"],["3-6-2-Rosetta-Settings","3.6.2 Rosetta Settings"],["3-6-3-Back-Up-to-USB","3.6.3 Back Up to USB"],["3-6-4-Update-Rosetta","3.6.4 Update Rosetta"],["3-7-Set-Rosetta-to-Auto-start-When-Starting-up-the-System","3.7 Set Rosetta to Auto-start When Starting up the System"],["3-8-Configure-the-Menus-Using-MenuLibre","3.8 Configure the Menus Using MenuLibre"],["3-8-1-Add-a-Rosetta-Menu","3.8.1 Add a Rosetta Menu"],["3-8-2-Add-the-Rosetta-Program","3.8.2 Add the Rosetta Program"],["3-8-3-Add-the-Rosetta-Settings-Configuration-Program","3.8.3 Add the Rosetta Settings Configuration Program"],["3-8-4-Add-the-Rosetta-Backup-Program","3.8.4 Add the Rosetta Backup Program"],["3-8-5-Add-the-Rosetta-Update-Program","3.8.5 Add the Rosetta Update Program"],["3-8-6-Hide-Some-Unnecessary-Programs-Program-Menus","3.8.6 Hide Some Unnecessary Programs & Program Menus"],["3-9-Turn-off-Hidden-Files-in-Thunar","3.9 Turn off Hidden Files in Thunar"],["3-10-Reboot","3.10 Reboot"],["Part-4-Install-Development-System-Applications","Part 4 - Install Development System Applications"],["4-1-Glade","4.1 Glade"],["4-2-GIMP","4.2 GIMP"],["Part-5-Other-Notes","Part 5 - Other Notes"],["5-1-To-SSH-In","5.1 To SSH In"],["5-2-To-Unzip","5.2 To Unzip"]],"x":"This page covers installing and setting up the Rosetta software on the Raspberry Pi, and setting up the Mesa cards in the base box and the optional expansion box. Build the hardware first - see Hardware Build . Prerequisites To get the software downloaded for installing and setting up Rosetta, you will need: Computer connected to the internet with Linux, macOS, or Windows. microSD card reader/writer on that computer. Authority on that computer to install Raspberry Pi Imager You will also need: The Rosetta hardware, built as described in Hardware Build , with each Mesa card's W5/W6 jumpers at the factory setting (both down). A WiFi network with internet access. Part 1 - Build the Boot Drive for LinuxCNC There are several distinct paths for setting up LinuxCNC on Rosetta. You may have access to a system that is already completely set up. Or you may install a pre-configured Rosetta installation onto an SD card. This document currently focus on the most bare option which begins with a standard LinuxCNC image and then customizes it for Rosetta. 1.1 Get the Raspberry Pi Imager Download and install the latest Raspberry Pi Imager (version 2.0.1 or later). This is available at https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/ The Raspberry Pi Imager is used to create the boot disk for the Raspberry Pi using the image downloaded in the next step. 1.2 Download the LinuxCNC Image File Download the latest file from http://linuxcnc.org/downloads . Be sure to get the one for the Raspberry Pi 5. It is probably not the 1st one on the list. KEY NOTE : When the system gets upgraded in a later step, it will upgrade the LinuxCNC to the latest version. 1.3 Create the Boot Disk for the Raspberry Pi Use the Raspberry Pi Imager downloaded onto the computer in step 1.1 above. Insert the microSD card into the computer, and ensure the computer sees it. The card will be completely overwritten. A 16G card is the smallest acceptable. Larger is desirable. Start the Raspberry Pi Imager . For the settings, use: Device Raspberry Pi 5 OS Use custom (the bottom selection). Select the file you just downloaded in the prior step. Storage This is for the microSD card you just added in step a, above. Be sure to not select your computer’s main hard drive. Writing This will show a summary. Click WRITE An alternative is to use usbimager to copy an existing SD card. Part 2 - Configure Linux on the Raspberry Pi A Word about Network Connections The Raspberry Pi has two network connections, and each has its own job: WiFi is the Raspberry Pi's connection to the internet (for setup, updates and web pages). The wired connection goes only to the Ethernet switch in the base box, which connects the Raspberry Pi to the Mesa cards. It has no gateway and no internet, and nothing else is plugged into that switch. A new Mesa card answers at its factory address, 192.168.1.121. So during setup the Raspberry Pi's wired connection starts on 192.168.1.120 to reach it. In Part 3, each card is given its permanent address, and the Raspberry Pi moves to 10.10.10.100: Part IP Address Initial Set To Raspberry Pi (wired) 192.168.1.120 10.10.10.100 Base card 192.168.1.121 10.10.10.10 Expansion card 192.168.1.121 10.10.10.11 2.1 Start the Raspberry Pi Install the microSD card into the Raspberry Pi and start the Raspberry Pi. When presented with the boot-up screen, continue. Do not try to configure the WiFi by clicking on the icon at the top right of the screen. That will mess up the startup process. 2.2 Set System Defaults Open a terminal emulator and enter this command: sudo passwd rpi-first-boot-wizard Set the password to something simple like “reuben”. You may not need this, but if the screen locks before everything is changed over to the new username, you may need this. In the pop-up window which came at start-up, select the values as outlined below: Country United States Language American English Timezone (whatever you wish) ☑ Use English Language ☑ Use US keyboard Click Next , and then enter this information. The username MUST be “ reuben ”! The system is built around this. Username reuben Password reuben Confirm Password reuben Click Next . Make no changes on that page. Click Next . Select the WiFi to use, and enter your WiFi password. Click Next . Click Next to take the option to update the software. Click Launch . The system will reboot at the end. 2.3 Set Wired Network Connection & Test Network Connections At this point, WiFi should be working. It is best to open the web browser and bring up this page. That way, you can copy the commands from the page, and paste them into a terminal emulator. The web site is https://rosetta.colvintools.com/Support . For this step, connect only the base box : the Raspberry Pi and the base card plugged into the switch, and the expansion box's Ethernet cable unplugged . Open a terminal emulator, and enter this command (it is a single command line). nmcli con add type ethernet con-name 'Wired Ethernet' ipv4.method manual ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.120/24 The automated install in Part 3 deletes and re-creates this connection, so it does no harm if you run this more than once. Test that everything works. 2.3.1 Test 1 Open a web browser and ensure you can open some web page, e.g., Google.com. This tests the WiFi connection. If it doesn't work, check the WiFi. Close the web browser. 2.3.2 Test 2 Ping the base card at its factory address by entering this command. Press Ctrl-C to stop it. ping 192.168.1.121 If there is no reply, check that the base box is switched on, the cables are plugged into the switch, and the card's W5/W6 jumpers are at the factory setting (both down). Close the terminal emulator. 2.4 Set Rest of System Options Open a terminal emulator, and enter this command: sudo raspi-config Select 1 System Options S4 Hostname Enter rosetta S6 Auto Login Select Yes for the question, “Would you like to automatically login to the console?” Select OK Select OK for the question, “Would you like to automatically login to the desktop?” Select OK Select 2 Display Options D2 Screen Blanking Select No for the question, “Would you like to enable screen blanking?” Select OK Select Finish For the question, “Would you like to reboot now?”, select Yes . 2.5 Download the Files Needed Open a terminal session, and enter these 5 commands (the git clone command is a single line): mkdir linuxcnc cd linuxcnc mkdir configs cd configs git clone https://github.com/RichColvin/Rosetta Once the files have been downloaded, they will be in /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta 2.6 Clean Up the Desktop Right-click on the desktop. Select Desktop Settings … Select the Desktop Icons tab Set the icon size to 100 Select font size, and set it to 18 Select the File/Launcher Icons tab Deselect all the default icons except for Removable Disks and Drives (which implies that Devices should also be left selected). This allows the user to insert a memory stick into the USB slot and see it shown on the desktop. Part 3 - Setup LinuxCNC W6 jumper location The automated install sets up the base box . If there is an expansion box, add it afterwards ( 3.2 ). If a script fails, every step can also be done by hand ( 3.3 ). The Raspberry Pi and the base card share one 5V power supply. Never power the card on by itself while the Raspberry Pi is running - that restarts the Raspberry Pi without shutting it down. When the jumpers are moved, shut down and switch off the whole machine. 3.1 Run the Automated Install (Base Box) Ensure the Raspberry Pi and the base card are powered on, and both are plugged into the switch, the expansion box (if any) is unplugged from the switch, the base card's W5/W6 jumpers are at the factory setting (both down), and WiFi is working (the install downloads programs from the internet). Open a terminal emulator, and enter this command: bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh The install asks these questions while it sets up the base card: “Connect ONLY the card being set up…” Check that only the base card is connected, then press Enter . “Write the Rosetta firmware to it (needed on a new card)?” y for a new card. N if the card already has the Rosetta firmware. “Store 10.10.10.10 as this card’s address?” y “Move the Pi’s wired connection to 10.10.10.100 now?” y “Delete them (the one in use is kept)?” Only asked if there are extra wired connections. y “Enter this system's serial number…” The number on the machine's label, e.g. 4 . Press Enter to skip - it is asked again at each update. “Shut the Pi down now?” (at the end) y The rest of the install runs by itself. It installs the programs listed in 3.3.3 , and adds the Rosetta and Gcode folder shortcuts (in the file manager's Places list, and as two Turk's head launchers on the bottom panel). 3.1.1 Move the Base Card's Jumpers At the end, the install shuts the Raspberry Pi down (if you answered y ). Then: Wait for the screen to go dark, then switch the machine off. On the base card, move the W6 jumper up (W5 stays down). This is the setting the 7i92 manual calls EEPROM : the card uses the address stored on it, 10.10.10.10. Switch the machine on. Once the Raspberry Pi has started, open a terminal emulator and enter: bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Change_Network.sh check It should say: Pi's wired connection (eth0): 10.10.10.100/24 Card at 10.10.10.10 answers. (It also says there is no card at 10.10.10.11. That is expected until the expansion box is added.) If the card doesn't answer, see 3.3.1, Step 7 . 3.2 Add the Expansion Box This step is only for a system with an expansion box. If one is not installed, proceed to step 3.4 . The expansion card is set up the same way as the base card, but is given the address 10.10.10.11. It also starts at the factory address 192.168.1.121, so the Raspberry Pi goes back to 192.168.1.120 while it is set up. Only the card being set up may be connected. Shut the Raspberry Pi down, and switch the machine off. Unplug the base card's Ethernet cable from the switch. Plug the expansion box's Ethernet cable into the switch. The expansion card's W5/W6 jumpers must be at the factory setting (both down). Switch the base box and the expansion box on. Once the Raspberry Pi has started, open a terminal emulator. Put the Raspberry Pi back on 192.168.1.120 (answer y to “Continue?”): bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Change_Network.sh back Set up the expansion card: bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Change_Network.sh expansion Answer the questions as in 3.1 . The address it offers to store is 10.10.10.11 . It then moves the Raspberry Pi to 10.10.10.100 and offers to shut it down - answer y . Wait for the screen to go dark, then switch the base box and the expansion box off. On the expansion card, move the W6 jumper up (W5 stays down) - the EEPROM setting. Plug the base card's Ethernet cable back into the switch. Switch both boxes on. Once the Raspberry Pi has started, enter: bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Change_Network.sh check It should say both Card at 10.10.10.10 answers. and Card at 10.10.10.11 answers. Open the Rosetta Settings program. On the Axis Selection tab, tick Expansion Box - Connected . Channels 06 - 09 can now be assigned. (The change takes effect the next time Rosetta starts.) 3.3 If a Script Fails - Doing It by Hand Use these steps if the automated install or the network script ( Rosetta_Change_Network.sh ) stops with an error. Start at the step where it stopped. Close Rosetta (LinuxCNC) first - the network steps can't be done while it is running. If no errors were encountered, proceed to step 3.4 . 3.3.1 Set Up a Mesa Card by Hand These are the commands Rosetta_Change_Network.sh runs. Use 10.10.10.10 for the base card or 10.10.10.11 for the expansion card wherever the steps say new address . Step 1 - Find the wired connection. List the connections: nmcli -f NAME,UUID,DEVICE con show There should be one connection called Wired Ethernet . If there is none, create it: sudo nmcli con add type ethernet con-name 'Wired Ethernet' ipv4.method manual ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.120/24 If there is more than one, delete the extras (keep the one with eth0 in the DEVICE column), using the UUID shown for each: sudo nmcli con delete <UUID> Step 2 - Put the Raspberry Pi on 192.168.1.120 (to reach a card at its factory address): sudo nmcli con mod \"Wired Ethernet\" ipv4.method manual ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.120/24 ipv4.gateway \"\" ipv4.dns \"\" sudo nmcli con up \"Wired Ethernet\" Step 3 - Check the card answers. Connect only the card being set up, with its W5/W6 jumpers at the factory setting (both down), and switch it on. Then: mesaflash --device 7i92t --addr 192.168.1.121 --readhmid This lists the card's details. If it says no board was found, check the cable, the card's power and its jumpers. Step 4 - Write the Rosetta firmware (a new card only): mesaflash --device 7i92t --addr 192.168.1.121 --write /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/MesaCard/7i92t_Rosetta.bin --reload Step 5 - Store the card's new address: mesaflash --device 7i92t --addr 192.168.1.121 --set ip=<new address> For example, --set ip=10.10.10.10 for the base card. (The option is --set ip= , with a space.) Step 6 - Move the Raspberry Pi to 10.10.10.100: sudo nmcli con mod \"Wired Ethernet\" ipv4.method manual ipv4.addresses 10.10.10.100/24 ipv4.gateway \"\" ipv4.dns \"\" connection.autoconnect-priority 10 sudo nmcli con up \"Wired Ethernet\" Step 7 - Shut down, move the jumper, and check. sudo poweroff Switch the machine off, move the card's W6 jumper up (W5 stays down), reconnect any card you unplugged, and switch the machine on. Then check each card: ip -4 addr show eth0 mesaflash --device 7i92t --addr 10.10.10.10 --readhmid mesaflash --device 7i92t --addr 10.10.10.11 --readhmid The first command should show inet 10.10.10.100/24 . If it doesn't, repeat Step 6. Each card that is set up and connected should list its details. 3.3.2 Undo: Put a Card or the Raspberry Pi Back A card: switch the machine off, put the card's W5/W6 jumpers back to the factory setting (both down), and switch on. It answers at 192.168.1.121 again. The Raspberry Pi: run bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Change_Network.sh back , or Step 2 above. 3.3.3 Automated Install Programs These programs should have been installed by the Automated Install program, Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh . If they are not, the commands are below for installing them. Copy the Mesa card files into /usr/lib/firmware/hm2/hostmot2 sudo mkdir -p /usr/lib/firmware/hm2/hostmot2 cd /usr/lib/firmware/hm2/hostmot2 sudo cp /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/MesaCard/7i92t_Rosetta.bin . sudo cp /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/MesaCard/7i92t_Rosetta.pin . sudo cp /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/MesaCard/PIN_Rosetta_34.vhd . Copy .axisrc into /home/reuben cd /home/reuben cp /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/axisrc .axisrc Create the gcode library cd /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta mkdir gcode Copy the starting settings file into Documents cd /home/reuben/Documents cp /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/RosettaSet_v1.ini . Install ClamAV cd /home/reuben mkdir ClamAv cd ClamAv mkdir SuspiciousFiles sudo apt install clamav Install menulibre sudo apt install menulibre Install galculator sudo apt install galculator Install xfce4-screenshooter sudo apt install xfce4-screenshooter Install Claude Code # Install Node 20 if you don't have it (Node 18+ required) curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_20.x | sudo -E bash - sudo apt install -y nodejs # Set up a user-level npm prefix (avoids sudo/permission mess) mkdir -p ~/.npm-global npm config set prefix '~/.npm-global' echo 'export PATH=~/.npm-global/bin:$PATH' >> ~/.bashrc source ~/.bashrc # Install Claude Code npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code claude --version Install Chromium sudo apt update sudo apt install chromium -y Add the folder shortcuts /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Add_Shortcuts.sh Record the serial number /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Set_Serial_Number.sh 3.4 Schedule the Virus Scan ClamAV keeps its virus signatures up to date by itself. Set up the weekly scan as described in Virus Scanning - Scheduling the Weekly Scan . 3.5 Set the Wallpaper Open the file manager. From the folder /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Images right-click on RosettaWallpaper.png then select Set as wallpaper . 3.6 Set the Icons on the Desktop 3.6.1 Rosetta Right-click on the desktop and select Create Launcher… . For the entries, enter: Name Rosetta Comment Desktop launcher for Rosetta Command bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Launch.sh Working Directory /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta Icon Click the No icon button, then Select icon from image files Select /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Images/Rosetta_Folder.png Options ☐ Use Startup notification ☐ Run in terminal Click Create . 3.6.2 Rosetta Settings Right-click on the desktop and select Create Launcher… . For the entries, enter: Name Rosetta Settings Comment Command bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Settings.sh Working Directory /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta Icon Click the No icon button, then Select icon from image files Select /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Images/Rosetta_SettingsGear.png Options ☐ Use Startup notification ☐ Run in terminal Click Create . 3.6.3 Back Up to USB Right-click on the desktop and select Create Launcher… . For the entries, enter: Name Back Up to USB Comment Command bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Backup.sh Working Directory /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta Icon Click the No icon button, then Select icon from image files Select /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Images/Rosetta_Backup.png Options ☐ Use Startup notification ☐ Run in terminal Click Create . 3.6.4 Update Rosetta Right-click on the desktop and select Create Launcher… . For the entries, enter: Name Update Rosetta Comment Command bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Update.sh Working Directory /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta Icon Click the No icon button, then Select icon from image files Select /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Images/Rosetta_Update.png Options ☐ Use Startup notification ✅ Run in terminal Click Create . The resulting desktop should look like this. 3.7 Set Rosetta to Auto-start When Starting up the System This is an optional step. Click on Applications , then Settings , and then Session and Startup . Click on the Application Autostart tab. Click +Add . For the entries, enter: Name Rosetta Description Command bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Launch.sh Trigger on login Click OK 3.8 Configure the Menus Using MenuLibre MenuLibre is a graphical editor for your desktop's application menu — it lets you control what shows up when you click the \"Applications\" or \"Start\" menu, and how. Open a terminal emulator, and enter this command (no sudo is required): menulibre 3.8.1 Add a Rosetta Menu Click the large + on the upper left part of the screen. Select Add Directory . Change “New Directory” to “Rosetta” Change “A small descriptive blurb about this directory” to “This is for the Rosetta programs.” Click on the folder icon, and replace it with Rosetta_Folder.png from the directory, /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Images/ . Click on the save button (the one beside the large + on the upper left part of the screen). Using the Up arrow at the bottom of the screen on the left, and move this menu to be above Terminal Emulator . 3.8.2 Add the Rosetta Program Click the large + on the upper left part of the screen. Select Add Launcher . Enter the values as outlined below. The image file is Rosetta_Folder.png. Rosetta Start the Rosetta system Application Details Command bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Launch.sh Working Directory /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta Options Run in terminal Use startup notification Hide from menus Click on the save button (the one beside the large + on the upper left part of the screen). 3.8.3 Add the Rosetta Settings Configuration Program Click the large + on the upper left part of the screen. Select Add Launcher . Enter the values as outlined below. The image file is Rosetta_SettingsGear.png. Rosetta Settings Run this to configure the way Rosetta works Application Details Command bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Settings.sh Working Directory /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta Options Run in terminal Use startup notification Hide from menus Click on the save button (the one beside the large + on the upper left part of the screen). 3.8.4 Add the Rosetta Backup Program Click the large + on the upper left part of the screen. Select Add Launcher . Enter the values as outlined below. The image file is Rosetta_Backup.png. Back Up to USB Run this to backup the key Rosetta user files Application Details Command bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Backup.sh Working Directory /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta Options Run in terminal Use startup notification Hide from menus Click on the save button (the one beside the large + on the upper left part of the screen). 3.8.5 Add the Rosetta Update Program Click the large + on the upper left part of the screen. Select Add Launcher . Enter the values as outlined below. The image file is Rosetta_Update.png. Update Rosetta Run this when notified to do so Application Details Command bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Update.sh Working Directory /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta Options Run in terminal Use startup notification Hide from menus Click on the save button (the one beside the large + on the upper left part of the screen). 3.8.6 Hide Some Unnecessary Programs & Program Menus For each of these applications, under Options, enable the option Hide from menus, saving each after the change Mail Reader Games Multimedia Office Science Click on the save button (the one beside the large + on the upper left part of the screen). The resulting MenuLibre screen should look like the picture below: MenuLibre Screen Which results in the Applications menu shown below. Applications Menu 3.9 Turn off Hidden Files in Thunar Open the File Manager (Thunar). Click View , then unclick Show Hidden Files . 3.10 Reboot Everything should be good now. Open a terminal emulator and enter this command: sudo reboot Part 4 - Install Development System Applications Install these applications on a development system. They are not needed on production systems, but can be installed if desired. 4.1 Glade Glade is used to develop the display panels used with GladeVCP. Open a terminal emulator, and enter these commands: sudo apt install glade 4.2 GIMP The GNU Image Manipulation Program is useful for editing image files. Open a terminal emulator, and enter these commands: sudo apt install gimp Part 5 - Other Notes 5.1 To SSH In To connect to the machine using SSH, use the command below, with the Raspberry Pi's WiFi address (the wired address, 10.10.10.100, can only be reached from the switch). To find the WiFi address, enter hostname -I on the Raspberry Pi. ssh reuben@<WiFi address of the Pi> 5.2 To Unzip sudo apt update sudo apt install file-roller"},{"u":"LaunchingRosetta.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Launching Rosetta","h":[["The-sequence","The sequence"],["Key-rules","Key rules"],["Which-card-address-is-used","Which card address is used"],["Roles-channels-and-joints","Roles, channels, and joints"]],"x":"Understanding this sequence makes most field problems much easier to track down. The sequence The desktop icon (and the Applications menu entry) runs Rosetta_Launch.sh . Rosetta_Launch.sh runs Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py . It first checks the gcode folder for reserved file names (starting with Rosetta, or M100 - M199) and stops if it finds one (see Rosetta_Launch.sh ). It then reads this machine's settings from /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini and writes these generated files next to their templates: Rosetta.local.ini - a copy of Rosetta.ini with the axis/channel assignment, jog speeds, measurement system, and backlash applied, and with its HALFILE / POSTGUI_HALFILE lines pointing at the files below; Rosetta.local.hal - a copy of Rosetta.hal with the Mesa card line rewritten (the base card at 10.10.10.10 alone, or both cards - 10.10.10.10 and 10.10.10.11 - when Expansion Box - Connected is ticked, plus the second card's read/write and watchdog lines), each role's block moved to its assigned channel, card and joint number, unassigned roles left out, and the limit jacks, E-stop button and signal tower wiring and the lost-card E-stop added (see Panel Accessories and Mesa 7i92 ); Rosetta_Display/Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.local.hal - a copy of Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.hal, plus the lines for the done/pause beeps and the fault siren; Rosetta_Display/Rosetta_Splash.local.ngc - the “Rosetta / ROSETTA” drawing shown in the preview at start-up (preview only - Run skips it). It then starts linuxcnc Rosetta.local.ini . While LinuxCNC loads, Rosetta_Settings_Restore.py applies the saved scale, PID and velocity values to the HAL pins. At shutdown, Rosetta_Shutdown.hal runs Rosetta_Scale_Persist.py , which saves the live values back to RosettaSet_v1.ini. Key rules Never edit the .local files - they are regenerated at every launch. Change RosettaSet_v1.ini (through the Settings program) or the templates instead. Everything that Rosetta.local.ini sets (axis assignment, units, jog speeds) takes effect only at the next launch. The Settings program only runs while LinuxCNC is not running. The generator prints what it did - which cards it loads (and at which addresses), which role is on which channel, which limit jacks, E-stop and signal tower are wired. Run it by hand to see that output: cd /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta python3 Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py If it reports a problem, it refuses to write the .local.hal files and Rosetta_Launch.sh stops, so LinuxCNC never starts with a half-built configuration. For example, it refuses to start with Expansion Box - Connected ticked while the Raspberry Pi's wired connection is still on the old 192.168.1.x network (see Mesa 7i92 ). Which card address is used The base card is loaded at 10.10.10.10 , and the expansion card at 10.10.10.11 . A machine not yet moved to the 10.10.10.x network (Raspberry Pi wired at 192.168.1.120) is loaded at the card's factory address, 192.168.1.121 , so an update can arrive before or after the network is moved with Rosetta_Change_Network.sh. Roles, channels, and joints See Axis Mapping ."},{"u":"Licensing.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Licensing & End User Customization","h":[["Overview","Overview"],["Licensing-and-End-User-Customization","Licensing and End User Customization"]],"x":"Overview The Rosetta system was developed by ornamental turners for use with a rose engine. It is designed to provide the ornamental turner a way for synchronizing activities on the rose engine using stepper motors, in a manner which also assists the ornamental turner with reducing the workload for onerous tasks. More information about the Rosetta system is available at: https://rosetta.colvintools.com/Rosetta_Overview.html . Its software is released under the MIT/X Consortium License below. The configuration is published on GitHub . Licensing and End User Customization The end user of the Rosetta system may customize it for their local configuration - the intended place for HAL additions is Rosetta_Custom/Rosetta_Custom.hal. However, changes to the Rosetta files are not supported by Colvin Tools nor Brainwave Embedded. The following MIT/X Consortium License applies to the Rosetta system. Use of this system constitutes consent to the terms outlined below. Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions: The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software. THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE. Except as contained in this notice, the name of COPYRIGHT HOLDERS shall not be used in advertising or otherwise to promote the sale, use or other dealings in this Software without prior written authorization from COPYRIGHT HOLDERS."},{"u":"MachineTracker.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Machine Tracker (Serial Numbers and Version Reports)","h":[["How-it-works","How it works"],["What-is-reported","What is reported"],["Files-on-the-machine","Files on the machine"],["Files-on-the-server","Files on the server"],["Common-tasks","Common tasks"],["Change-a-machine-s-serial-number","Change a machine's serial number"],["A-machine-s-Raspberry-Pi-was-replaced","A machine's Raspberry Pi was replaced"],["Remove-old-or-test-reports","Remove old or test reports"],["A-machine-doesn-t-appear-in-the-tracker","A machine doesn't appear in the tracker"],["Change-the-Machine-Tracker-password","Change the Machine Tracker password"],["Privacy","Privacy"]],"x":"S/N on the both screens Key Note: ① A user ID & password are required to access this report. This report is only available to ColvinTools. Each Rosetta machine reports its serial number and the Rosetta O/S version it is running every time it is updated. The reports are collected on the support manual web site, so we can see which machines are still in use, and which Rosetta O/S version each Rosetta system runs. This will be quite useful when an owner calls for support. This page covers the whole process, from setting a machine's serial number to managing the reports on the server. To access the on-line report, use this web link: Rosetta O/S Version Report . Key Note: ② Each Rosetta system is identified by its Raspberry Pi's own hardware ID, not by the Rosetta serial number assigned to it when the system is built. The serial number is only a label. Changing a machine's serial number does not create a second machine in the tracker - its row simply shows the new serial number assigned to that system after its next update. How it works The serial number is set on the machine. The installer (Step 11) asks for it. Machines built before this feature are asked during their next update. It can be skipped; the machine then asks again at every update until a number is entered. Every update sends a report. After Update Rosetta ( Rosetta_Update.sh ) pulls the latest files, Rosetta_Update_Settings.py runs Rosetta_Report_Version.py , which builds the report and sends it to the server. The machine keeps its own copy. Every report is written to a log on the machine first, whether or not it reaches the server. The server stores it. reb_report.php (on the server) adds each report it receives, as one line, to reb_reports.jsonl (on the server) . The tracker shows it. reb_report_view.php (the Machine Tracker ) shows one row per machine - its most recent report - newest first. A user ID & password are required to access this report. A machine that is offline when it updates simply isn't reported that time. A failed report never stops the update. The report is not sent again later; it goes at the next update. What is reported Field What it is hardware_id The Raspberry Pi's own serial number, built into the board (read from /proc/cpuinfo). Every Pi's is different, so it identifies the machine - including a home-built one - with no chance of two machines clashing. It changes only if the Pi itself is replaced (see below). serial_number The Rosetta serial number entered by the owner or builder, e.g. 4 for S/N 4. A label for talking to support - it doesn't have to be unique. Empty if it was never set. version and version_date The Rosetta O/S version and date shown at the bottom of the main panel, e.g. 1.0 beta 1.1 and 23 October 2026 . reported_at When the machine sent the report, by the machine's own clock (local time). Nothing else is sent - no owner name, no settings, no G-code. Files on the machine File Purpose /home/reuben/.config/Rosetta/ serial The machine's serial number - one line, just the number. /home/reuben/.config/Rosetta/ report_log.jsonl Every report this machine has built, one per line, whether or not it reached the server. Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Set_Serial_Number.sh Asks for the serial number if none is recorded; does nothing if one already is. Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Report_Version.py Builds the report, logs it, and sends it to the server. Files on the server These are on the support web site's hosting (GoDaddy), in the Support Manual's own folder, rosetta.colvintools.com/Support/ (until 04 November 2026, the top folder of support.roseenginebutler.com). They are not in the GitHub repository; they are changed by hand through GoDaddy's File Manager (or FTP). File Purpose reb_report.php Receives each machine's report and adds it to reb_reports.jsonl. reb_reports.jsonl Every report ever received, one per line, oldest first. Never edited by the programs - only added to. Back this up before editing it by hand. reb_report_view.php The Machine Tracker page. .htaccess Makes the Machine Tracker ask for a password, and stops anyone opening reb_reports.jsonl or .htpasswd directly in a browser. .htpasswd The Machine Tracker's user name ( REBmachines ) and encrypted password. Files starting with a dot are hidden by default in GoDaddy's File Manager - turn on Show hidden files in its settings to see .htaccess and .htpasswd. Common tasks Change a machine's serial number On the machine, in a terminal (replace NewSN with the new serial number to be assigned to this Rosetta system): echo NewSN > ~/.config/Rosetta/serial Then either run Update Rosetta , or send a report straight away: python3 ~/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Report_Version.py Nothing needs to change on the server. The machine keeps the same row in the Machine Tracker, which now shows the new number. The older lines in reb_reports.jsonl keep the old number; they can be left alone (see Remove old or test reports to tidy them). A machine's Raspberry Pi was replaced The new Pi has a different hardware ID, so after its next update the machine appears as a new row, with the same serial number as the old one. The old row stays, showing the date of the old Pi's last report. Remove the old Pi's lines from reb_reports.jsonl (below) once you're sure it's no longer in use. Remove old or test reports In GoDaddy's File Manager, download a copy of reb_reports.jsonl as a backup. Open it with the File Manager's editor. Each line is one report, e.g. {\"hardware_id\": \"10000000abcd1234\", \"serial_number\": \"4\", \"version\": \"1.0 beta 1.1\", \"version_date\": \"23 October 2026\", \"reported_at\": \"2026-10-24 09:15:02\"} Delete the lines you don't want - every line with that machine's hardware_id removes the machine from the tracker entirely. Delete whole lines only, and leave no blank lines between the others. Save, and reload the Machine Tracker to check. A machine doesn't appear in the tracker Check it has been updated since 23 October 2026 (when reporting started). On the machine, run the report by hand (the command under Change a machine's serial number ). It prints Version report sent to ... when it works, or a WARNING saying why not - usually no internet connection. Look at the end of /home/reuben/.config/Rosetta/ report_log.jsonl to see what the machine has been sending. Change the Machine Tracker password Create a new .htpasswd line for user REBmachines with an htpasswd generator (or the htpasswd command on a Pi), and replace the line in .htpasswd with it. Keep the password somewhere safe - it isn't recorded anywhere else. Privacy The tracker holds no names or addresses - only the Pi's hardware ID, the serial number, the version and the date. Matching a serial number to an owner is done from Colvin Tools' own build records, not here. reb_reports.jsonl and .htpasswd can't be opened from a browser, and the Machine Tracker needs the password."},{"u":"Mesa7i92.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Mesa 7i92 Ethernet Anything I/O Card","h":[["Key-Notes","Key Notes"],["Setting-up-a-card","Setting up a card"],["To-load-the-configuration-onto-a-Mesa-card-by-hand","To load the configuration onto a Mesa card by hand"],["To-display-the-configuration-on-a-Mesa-card","To display the configuration on a Mesa card"],["How-LinuxCNC-loads-the-cards","How LinuxCNC loads the cards"]],"x":"Key Notes This covers the 7i92TH and 7i92TM boards. A Rosetta has one card in the base box and, optionally, a second card in the expansion box. Both run the same firmware (7i92t_Rosetta.bin) and are set up the same way; they differ only in their address: Card Address HAL name Step generators used Base card 10.10.10.10 hm2_7i92.0 6 (channels 00 - 05) Expansion card 10.10.10.11 hm2_7i92.1 4 (channels 06 - 09) Raspberry Pi (wired) 10.10.10.100 A new card always answers at its factory address, 192.168.1.121 (W5 and W6 jumpers both down). It answers at its stored address only after its W6 jumper is moved up (W5 stays down) - the setting the 7i92 manual calls EEPROM. Jumpers are read only at power-up. Because both new cards answer at the same factory address, only the card being set up may be connected to the switch. The Raspberry Pi and the base card share one 5V supply. Never switch the card on by itself while the Raspberry Pi is running - that restarts the Raspberry Pi without shutting it down. Shut down and switch the whole machine off to move a jumper. Recovery: putting a card's W5/W6 jumpers back down returns it to 192.168.1.121. Setting up a card Use Rosetta_Change_Network.sh , which asks before every change. The full steps are in Installation and Setup for Debian 13 Trixie (Part 3), including how to do each step by hand. bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Change_Network.sh choice Choice What it does base Set up the connected card as the base card (10.10.10.10). expansion Set up the connected card as the expansion card (10.10.10.11). pi Move the Raspberry Pi's wired connection to 10.10.10.100 (no gateway or DNS). back Put the Raspberry Pi's wired connection back on 192.168.1.120, to reach a card at its factory address. check Show the Raspberry Pi's wired address and which cards answer. Add --dry-run to see the commands without running them. The script stops if LinuxCNC is running. To load the configuration onto a Mesa card by hand With only that card connected, at its factory address: cd /usr/lib/firmware/hm2/hostmot2 mesaflash --device 7i92t --addr 192.168.1.121 --write 7i92t_Rosetta.bin --reload To display the configuration on a Mesa card Use the card's address - 10.10.10.10 (base), 10.10.10.11 (expansion), or 192.168.1.121 for a card not yet set up: mesaflash --device 7i92t --addr 10.10.10.10 --readhmid mesaflash --device 7i92t --addr 10.10.10.11 --readhmid How LinuxCNC loads the cards Both cards are loaded on one loadrt hm2_eth line (a realtime module can only be loaded once), e.g. board_ip=\"10.10.10.10,10.10.10.11\" with one config= string for each card (6 and 4 step generators). This gives hm2_7i92.0 and hm2_7i92.1, in that order. Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py writes that line at every launch: the base card alone, or both cards when Expansion Box - Connected is ticked in the Settings program. It also adds the second card's read/write functions and its timer and watchdog settings. While the Raspberry Pi's wired connection is still on 192.168.1.x (a machine not yet moved to 10.10.10.x), the generator loads the base card at 192.168.1.121 instead, and refuses to start with the expansion box ticked. Servo-thread order (from Mesa): both cards' read-requests, then their reads, then everything else, with the cards' writes last. If either card loses contact (cable pulled, card switched off), LinuxCNC goes into E-stop - see Field Troubleshooting . With two cards the 1 ms servo thread is busier (worst about 0.55 ms measured on the prototype, jogging on both cards). If LinuxCNC reports an unexpected realtime delay, see Field Troubleshooting . See also, https://linuxcnc.org/docs/stable/html/drivers/hostmot2.html ."},{"u":"MesaBoardPinToAxisMapping.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Mesa 7i92 Board Pin to Axis Mapping","h":[["Base-Box","Base Box"],["Connector-P2","Connector P2"],["Connector-P1","Connector P1"],["Expansion-Box","Expansion Box"],["Connector-P2_","Connector P2"],["Connector-P1_","Connector P1"],["Notes","Notes"]],"x":"Base Box Connector P2 HDR 26 Pin# I/O Pri. func Sec. func Chan nel Sec. Pin func Sec. Pin Dir Axis Use Comment 1 0 IO17 StepGen 00 Step/Table1 (Out) Chan 00 Step/Pulse 2 1 IO18 StepGen 00 Dir/Table2 (Out) Chan 00 Dir 3 2 IO19 InM 00 Input0_EncA0 (In) Limit Jack 1 Limit Switch (NC) hm2_7i92.0.gpio.002.in ① 4 3 IO20 OutM 00 Output0 (Out) Chan 00 ENAble hm2_7i92.0.outm.00.out-00 5 4 IO21 StepGen 01 Step/Table1 (Out) Chan 01 Step/Pulse 6 5 IO22 StepGen 01 Dir/Table2 (Out) Chan 01 Dir 7 6 IO23 InM 00 Input1_EncB0 (In) Limit Jack 2 Limit Switch (NC) hm2_7i92.0.gpio.006.in ① 8 7 IO24 OutM 00 Output1 (Out) Chan 01 ENAble hm2_7i92.0.outm.00.out-01 9 8 IO25 StepGen 02 Step/Table1 (Out) Chan 02 Step/Pulse 10 GND 11 9 IO26 StepGen 02 Dir/Table2 (Out) Chan 02 Dir 12 GND 13 10 IO27 InM 00 Input2_EncA1 (In) Limit Jack 3 Limit Switch (NC) hm2_7i92.0.gpio.010.in ① 14 GND 15 11 IO28 OutM 00 Output2 (Out) Chan 02 ENAble hm2_7i92.0.outm.00.out-02 16 GND 17 12 IO29 StepGen 03 Step/Table1 (Out) Chan 03 Step/Pulse 18 GND Jumper W4 is DOWN = breakout power disabled (default) ③ 19 13 IO30 StepGen 03 Dir/Table2 (Out) Chan 03 Dir 20 GND Jumper W4 is DOWN = breakout power disabled (default) ③ 21 14 IO31 InM 00 Input3_EncB1 (In) Spare input 22 GND Jumper W4 is DOWN = breakout power disabled (default) ③ 23 15 IO32 OutM 00 Output3 (Out) Chan 03 ENAble hm2_7i92.0.outm.00.out-03 24 GND Jumper W4 is DOWN = breakout power disabled (default) ③ 25 16 IO33 None Signal Tower Red light relay (out) hm2_7i92.0.gpio.016.out ④ , ⑤ 26 Connector P1 HDR 26 Pin# I/O Pri. func Sec. func Chan nel Sec. Pin func Sec. Pin Dir Axis Use Comment 1 17 IO0 StepGen 04 Step/Table1 (Out) Chan 04 Step/Pulse 2 18 IO1 StepGen 04 Dir/Table2 (Out) Chan 04 Dir 3 19 IO2 InM 00 Input4_EncA2 (In) Spare input 4 20 IO3 OutM 00 Output4 (Out) Chan 04 ENAble hm2_7i92.0.outm.00.out-04 5 21 IO4 StepGen 05 Step/Table1 (Out) Chan 05 Step/Pulse 6 22 IO5 StepGen 05 Dir/Table2 (Out) Chan 05 Dir 7 23 IO6 InM 00 Input5_EncB2 (In) Spare input 8 24 IO7 OutM 00 Output5 (Out) Chan 05 ENAble hm2_7i92.0.outm.00.out-05 9 25 IO8 StepGen 06 Step/Table1 (Out) (unused) Step/Pulse Previously used for Chan 06 when an 8-channel system was used 10 GND 11 26 IO9 StepGen 06 Dir/Table2 (Out) (unused) Dir Previously used for Chan 06 when an 8-channel system was used 12 GND 13 27 IO10 InM 00 Input6_EncA3 (In) Signal Tower Buzzer relay (out) hm2_7i92.0.gpio.027.out ④ , ⑦ 14 GND 15 28 IO11 OutM 00 Output6 (Out) (unused) ENAble Previously used for Chan 06 when an 8-channel system was used (hm2_7i92.0.outm.00.out-06) 16 GND 17 29 IO12 StepGen 07 Step/Table1 (Out) (unused) Step/Pulse Previously used for Chan 07 when an 8-channel system was used 18 5V Jumper W3 is UP = breakout power enabled ③ 19 30 IO13 StepGen 07 Dir/Table2 (Out) (unused) Dir Previously used for Chan 07 when an 8-channel system was used 20 5V Jumper W3 is UP = breakout power enabled ③ 21 31 IO14 InM 00 Input7_EncB3 (In) E-Stop Button E-Stop (NC) hm2_7i92.0.gpio.031.in ② 22 5V Jumper W3 is UP = breakout power enabled ③ 23 32 IO15 OutM 00 Output7 (Out) (unused) ENAble Previously used for Chan 07 when an 8-channel system was used (hm2_7i92.0.outm.00.out-07) 24 5V Jumper W3 is UP = breakout power enabled ③ 25 33 IO16 None Signal Tower Green light relay (out) hm2_7i92.0.gpio.033.out ④ , ⑥ 26 Expansion Box Connector P2 HDR 26 Pin# I/O Pri. func Sec. func Chan nel Sec. Pin func Sec. Pin Dir Axis Use Comment 1 0 IO17 StepGen 00 Step/Table1 (Out) Chan 06 Step/Pulse 2 1 IO18 StepGen 00 Dir/Table2 (Out) Chan 06 Dir 3 2 IO19 InM 00 Input0_EncA0 (In) Limit Jack 4 Limit Switch (NC) hm2_7i92.1.gpio.002.in ① 4 3 IO20 OutM 00 Output0 (Out) Chan 06 ENAble hm2_7i92.1.outm.00.out-00 5 4 IO21 StepGen 01 Step/Table1 (Out) Chan 07 Step/Pulse 6 5 IO22 StepGen 01 Dir/Table2 (Out) Chan 07 Dir 7 6 IO23 InM 00 Input1_EncB0 (In) Limit Jack 5 Limit Switch (NC) hm2_7i92.1.gpio.006.in ① 8 7 IO24 OutM 00 Output1 (Out) Chan 07 ENAble hm2_7i92.1.outm.00.out-01 9 8 IO25 StepGen 02 Step/Table1 (Out) Chan 08 Step/Pulse 10 GND 11 9 IO26 StepGen 02 Dir/Table2 (Out) Chan 08 Dir 12 GND 13 10 IO27 InM 00 Input2_EncA1 (In) Limit Jack 6 Limit Switch (NC) hm2_7i92.1.gpio.010.in ① 14 GND 15 11 IO28 OutM 00 Output2 (Out) Chan 08 ENAble hm2_7i92.1.outm.00.out-02 16 GND 17 12 IO29 StepGen 03 Step/Table1 (Out) Chan 09 Step/Pulse 18 GND Jumper W4 is DOWN = breakout power disabled (default) ③ 19 13 IO30 StepGen 03 Dir/Table2 (Out) Chan 09 Dir 20 GND Jumper W4 is DOWN = breakout power disabled (default) ③ 21 14 IO31 InM 00 Input3_EncB1 (In) Spare input 22 GND Jumper W4 is DOWN = breakout power disabled (default) ③ 23 15 IO32 OutM 00 Output3 (Out) Chan 09 ENAble hm2_7i92.1.outm.00.out-03 24 GND Jumper W4 is DOWN = breakout power disabled (default) ③ 25 16 IO33 None (Unused) 26 Connector P1 Connector P1 is not used for this design. Notes Limit switches GX-12/2 plug Normally Closed (NC) switches are recommended. Limit Jacks 1 - 3 are on the base card; Limit Jacks 4 - 6 are on the expansion card and work only while Expansion Box - Connected is ticked. When set to Normally Open (NO) in the Rosetta Settings program, LinuxCNC reads the card's inverted input for that pin ( .in_not instead of .in ), so no NOT function is needed. See also, Limit Switch Cabling E-Stop Button GX-16/2 plug E-stop button must be a Normally Closed (NC) switch See also, E-stop Button Cabling Breakout Power W3 switches the 5V breakout power on connector P1, and W4 on connector P2. For more information, see 7I92 Ethernet Anything I/O Manual , pg. 2 Signal Tower GX-12/4 plug Active low from 7i92 See also, Signal Tower Signal Tower Cabling Signal Tower Relays Signal Tower Red Light On after a following error, a limit trip, or a Mesa card losing contact; stays on until the machine is next switched on (F2). Signal Tower Green Light On while a program or MDI command runs, and while a spindle is running or indexing. Signal Tower Buzzer The 3-second fault siren (when red comes on), and the done / pause beeps - each can be set to the tower, the speaker, both, or off in the Settings program."},{"u":"PIDTuning.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Panel Accessories - Limit Switches, E-Stop Button, Signal Tower","h":[["Mesa-7i92-pins","Mesa 7i92 pins"],["Limit-switches","Limit switches"],["E-stop-button","E-stop button"],["Signal-tower","Signal tower"],["Checking-and-fixing","Checking and fixing"]],"x":"This is the technical view of the optional panel accessories. For cabling and operator settings, see the User Manual: Limit Switch Cabling , E-Stop Button Cabling , Signal Tower Cabling , and Axis Selection (User Manual). All three are off by default , are switched on in the Settings program ( Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds tab), and are wired into Rosetta.local.hal at the next launch by Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py (see Launching Rosetta ). The E-stop button, the signal tower and Limit Jacks 1 - 3 are on the base box . Limit Jacks 4 - 6 are on the optional expansion box and are used only while Expansion Box - Connected is ticked (Axis Selection tab); otherwise they are greyed out, keep their choice, and are left out of the configuration. Mesa 7i92 pins The generator loads the cards with num_inms=0 , so the firmware's InM input pins are plain GPIO ( hm2_7i92.N.gpio.NNN.in / .out , N = the card - 0 base, 1 expansion - and NNN = the 3-digit I/O number). No firmware change is needed. Function Box Header pin I/O HAL pin Setting in the generator Limit Jack 1 / 2 / 3 Base P2 pins 3, 7, 13 002, 006, 010 hm2_7i92.0.gpio.002.in / .006.in / .010.in LIMIT_JACK_INPUT Limit Jack 4 / 5 / 6 Expansion P2 pins 3, 7, 13 002, 006, 010 hm2_7i92.1.gpio.002.in / .006.in / .010.in LIMIT_JACK_INPUT E-stop button Base P1 pin 21 031 hm2_7i92.0.gpio.031.in ESTOP_BUTTON_INPUT Signal tower green Base P1 pin 25 033 hm2_7i92.0.gpio.033.out SIGNAL_TOWER_OUTPUT Signal tower red Base P2 pin 25 016 hm2_7i92.0.gpio.016.out SIGNAL_TOWER_OUTPUT Signal tower buzzer Base P1 pin 13 027 hm2_7i92.0.gpio.027.out SIGNAL_TOWER_OUTPUT Spare on the base card: Input3 (P2 pin 21), Input4 (P1 pin 3), Input5 (P1 pin 7). Spare on the expansion card: Input3 (P2 pin 21); its connector P1 is not used. Full map: Mesa 7i92 Pin Mapping . Limit switches Normally-closed switch between the input and GND; the 7i92's pull-up makes an open switch (tripped, broken, or unplugged) read TRUE = limit hit. With Limit Switch Type set to NO in the Settings program, the generator reads .in_not instead, for all six jacks. NC is strongly recommended. Each in-use jack drives a net named after its axis: <letter>-limit-sw (both ends - feeds the joint's neg-lim-sw-in and pos-lim-sw-in), <letter>-limit-min (neg-lim-sw-in only) or <letter>-limit-max (pos-lim-sw-in only). Rosetta.hal itself never touches the joints' limit pins. A jack can protect any axis in use, on either box - e.g. Limit Jack 1 on the base box can guard an axis on the expansion box. A jack whose axis isn't assigned to a channel in use, or that conflicts with a lower-numbered jack, is skipped with a message in the generator's output. E-stop button LinuxCNC's E-stop chain always runs through estop-latch.0 : iocontrol.0.user-enable-out -> ok-in, user-request-enable -> reset, ok-out -> iocontrol.0.emc-enable-in. The latch's fault-in is an OR ( estop-fault-or ) of the E-stop button (when marked connected) and card-fault - any Mesa card losing contact (its packet-error-exceeded or watchdog.has_bit ). Pressing the button (or a broken / unplugged cable) latches E-stop until the button is released and F1 is pressed. A lost card latches E-stop the same way; F1 works once the card answers again (see Field Troubleshooting ). Signal tower Green = a logic OR (tower-green-or) of: not halui.program.is-idle (a program or MDI command is running - Sync Move, Threading, indexing), each active spindle running (spindle.N.forward / reverse), and each active spindle indexing (orient enabled and not yet done). Red = a latch: every active joint's joint.N.f-errored, every in-use limit net and card-fault (a Mesa card losing contact) are OR'd by tower-fault-or into tower-red-hold , a oneshot with a practically endless pulse that stays on until the machine is next switched on (F2). LinuxCNC clears f-errored as the machine turns off, and a limit switch closes again once the axis stops, so without the latch red would only flash. A limit switch still open after F2 keeps red lit ( tower-red-or ) until the axis is moved off it. If the base card loses contact the tower stays dark - its outputs are on that card. The panel message and E-stop still happen. Buzzer = a 3-second oneshot (tower-buzz) on red's rising edge (SIGNAL_TOWER_BUZZER_SECONDS). The fault siren can also, or instead, play on the Raspberry Pi's speaker (Settings program, Sounds). The buzzer also gives the done / pause beeps (3 short beeps when a program, Run Operation or Cut Thread finishes; 2 at M0 / M1 / M60), through tower-buzzer-or . Relays switch ON when pulled LOW - the Rosetta standard, fixed in the software (SIGNAL_TOWER_ACTIVE_LOW). The 7i92 holds its pins high whenever LinuxCNC isn't driving them (boot, after exit), so an active-low board stays quiet then. With the tower switched off, the three pins stay inputs. Checking and fixing Watch an input in halshow (e.g., hm2_7i92.0.gpio.002.in, or hm2_7i92.1.gpio.002.in for Limit Jack 4): it should read FALSE with the switch closed and TRUE when pressed or unplugged. If every limit input reads backwards, check the Limit Switch Type (NC / NO) setting. If the tower lights or buzzer are on at boot or after LinuxCNC exits, the relay board is active-high. That isn't supported - use relays that switch on when their input is pulled low (as on the Rosetta circuit board). If a min switch stops the axis at the max end, swap min/max for that axis's jacks in the Settings program (a motor with reversed direction swaps them). Limit Jacks 4 - 6 do nothing: check Expansion Box - Connected is ticked and the expansion card answers (see Field Troubleshooting )."},{"u":"PanelAccessories.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Panel Accessories - Limit Switches, E-Stop Button, Signal Tower","h":[["Mesa-7i92-pins","Mesa 7i92 pins"],["Limit-switches","Limit switches"],["E-stop-button","E-stop button"],["Signal-tower","Signal tower"],["Checking-and-fixing","Checking and fixing"]],"x":"This is the technical view of the optional panel accessories. For cabling and operator settings, see the User Manual: Limit Switch Cabling , E-Stop Button Cabling , Signal Tower Cabling , and Axis Selection (User Manual). All three are off by default , are switched on in the Settings program ( Inputs, Outputs, and Sounds tab), and are wired into Rosetta.local.hal at the next launch by Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py (see Launching Rosetta ). The E-stop button, the signal tower and Limit Jacks 1 - 3 are on the base box . Limit Jacks 4 - 6 are on the optional expansion box and are used only while Expansion Box - Connected is ticked (Axis Selection tab); otherwise they are greyed out, keep their choice, and are left out of the configuration. Mesa 7i92 pins The generator loads the cards with num_inms=0 , so the firmware's InM input pins are plain GPIO ( hm2_7i92.N.gpio.NNN.in / .out , N = the card - 0 base, 1 expansion - and NNN = the 3-digit I/O number). No firmware change is needed. Function Box Header pin I/O HAL pin Setting in the generator Limit Jack 1 / 2 / 3 Base P2 pins 3, 7, 13 002, 006, 010 hm2_7i92.0.gpio.002.in / .006.in / .010.in LIMIT_JACK_INPUT Limit Jack 4 / 5 / 6 Expansion P2 pins 3, 7, 13 002, 006, 010 hm2_7i92.1.gpio.002.in / .006.in / .010.in LIMIT_JACK_INPUT E-stop button Base P1 pin 21 031 hm2_7i92.0.gpio.031.in ESTOP_BUTTON_INPUT Signal tower green Base P1 pin 25 033 hm2_7i92.0.gpio.033.out SIGNAL_TOWER_OUTPUT Signal tower red Base P2 pin 25 016 hm2_7i92.0.gpio.016.out SIGNAL_TOWER_OUTPUT Signal tower buzzer Base P1 pin 13 027 hm2_7i92.0.gpio.027.out SIGNAL_TOWER_OUTPUT Spare on the base card: Input3 (P2 pin 21), Input4 (P1 pin 3), Input5 (P1 pin 7). Spare on the expansion card: Input3 (P2 pin 21); its connector P1 is not used. Full map: Mesa 7i92 Pin Mapping . Limit switches Normally-closed switch between the input and GND; the 7i92's pull-up makes an open switch (tripped, broken, or unplugged) read TRUE = limit hit. With Limit Switch Type set to NO in the Settings program, the generator reads .in_not instead, for all six jacks. NC is strongly recommended. Each in-use jack drives a net named after its axis: <letter>-limit-sw (both ends - feeds the joint's neg-lim-sw-in and pos-lim-sw-in), <letter>-limit-min (neg-lim-sw-in only) or <letter>-limit-max (pos-lim-sw-in only). Rosetta.hal itself never touches the joints' limit pins. A jack can protect any axis in use, on either box - e.g. Limit Jack 1 on the base box can guard an axis on the expansion box. A jack whose axis isn't assigned to a channel in use, or that conflicts with a lower-numbered jack, is skipped with a message in the generator's output. E-stop button LinuxCNC's E-stop chain always runs through estop-latch.0 : iocontrol.0.user-enable-out -> ok-in, user-request-enable -> reset, ok-out -> iocontrol.0.emc-enable-in. The latch's fault-in is an OR ( estop-fault-or ) of the E-stop button (when marked connected) and card-fault - any Mesa card losing contact (its packet-error-exceeded or watchdog.has_bit ). Pressing the button (or a broken / unplugged cable) latches E-stop until the button is released and F1 is pressed. A lost card latches E-stop the same way; F1 works once the card answers again (see Field Troubleshooting ). Signal tower Green = a logic OR (tower-green-or) of: not halui.program.is-idle (a program or MDI command is running - Sync Move, Threading, indexing), each active spindle running (spindle.N.forward / reverse), and each active spindle indexing (orient enabled and not yet done). Red = a latch: every active joint's joint.N.f-errored, every in-use limit net and card-fault (a Mesa card losing contact) are OR'd by tower-fault-or into tower-red-hold , a oneshot with a practically endless pulse that stays on until the machine is next switched on (F2). LinuxCNC clears f-errored as the machine turns off, and a limit switch closes again once the axis stops, so without the latch red would only flash. A limit switch still open after F2 keeps red lit ( tower-red-or ) until the axis is moved off it. If the base card loses contact the tower stays dark - its outputs are on that card. The panel message and E-stop still happen. Buzzer = a 3-second oneshot (tower-buzz) on red's rising edge (SIGNAL_TOWER_BUZZER_SECONDS). The fault siren can also, or instead, play on the Raspberry Pi's speaker (Settings program, Sounds). The buzzer also gives the done / pause beeps (3 short beeps when a program, Run Operation or Cut Thread finishes; 2 at M0 / M1 / M60), through tower-buzzer-or . Relays switch ON when pulled LOW - the Rosetta standard, fixed in the software (SIGNAL_TOWER_ACTIVE_LOW). The 7i92 holds its pins high whenever LinuxCNC isn't driving them (boot, after exit), so an active-low board stays quiet then. With the tower switched off, the three pins stay inputs. Checking and fixing Watch an input in halshow (e.g., hm2_7i92.0.gpio.002.in, or hm2_7i92.1.gpio.002.in for Limit Jack 4): it should read FALSE with the switch closed and TRUE when pressed or unplugged. If every limit input reads backwards, check the Limit Switch Type (NC / NO) setting. If the tower lights or buzzer are on at boot or after LinuxCNC exits, the relay board is active-high. That isn't supported - use relays that switch on when their input is pulled low (as on the Rosetta circuit board). If a min switch stops the axis at the max end, swap min/max for that axis's jacks in the Settings program (a motor with reversed direction swaps them). Limit Jacks 4 - 6 do nothing: check Expansion Box - Connected is ticked and the expansion card answers (see Field Troubleshooting )."},{"u":"RosettaBackup.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Rosetta_Backup.sh - Back Up to USB","h":[["Purpose","Purpose"],["How-it-is-run","How it is run"],["Parameters","Parameters"],["Using-it","Using it"],["What-it-copies","What it copies"],["Files-it-creates","Files it creates"],["Restore-from-USB","Restore from USB"],["Programs-it-calls","Programs it calls"],["Notes-for-support","Notes for support"]],"x":"Rosetta’s Key Programs: Overview · Rosetta_Launch.sh · Rosetta_Settings.sh · Rosetta_Backup.sh · Rosetta_Update.sh · Rosetta_Change_Network.sh · Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh Purpose Rosetta_Backup.sh starts Back Up to USB , a small window that copies the operator's own files onto a USB memory stick: the settings, G-code programs, custom HAL file and tool table. Everything else can be reinstalled from GitHub, so these are the files that matter if the Raspberry Pi's SD card fails. The same program has a Restore from USB window, which copies a backup back onto the Raspberry Pi. It is only started from Rosetta Settings' File menu - see Restore from USB below. How it is run The Backup Rosetta desktop icon, and Applications > Rosetta > Backup Rosetta. Rosetta Settings' File menu > Back Up to USB... From a terminal: /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Backup.sh Parameters None. Using it Insert a USB stick. It appears in the window within a couple of seconds (one opened before the stick goes in is fine). Choose the stick if more than one is shown, and click Back Up . Wait for Safe to remove before pulling the stick out. The stick has been ejected: to use it again (another backup, a restore), pull it out and plug it back in. What it copies From the Raspberry Pi To the stick /home/reuben/Documents/ *.ini (RosettaSet_v1.ini and saved or exported settings) Documents/ /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/ gcode/ (all files and subfolders) gcode/ .../Rosetta/Rosetta_Custom/ Rosetta_Custom.hal and Rosetta_Tool.tbl Rosetta_Custom/ /home/reuben/linuxcnc/ nc_files/ (all files and subfolders; links to LinuxCNC's own examples are skipped) nc_files/ A folder missing on this machine is skipped and noted in the log. The list is BACKUP_SOURCES at the top of Rosetta_Display/Rosetta_Backup.py. Files it creates On the stick, a new folder every time - an earlier backup is never overwritten: Rosetta_Backup/YYYY-MM-DD_HHMM/ Rosetta_Backup/YYYY-MM-DD_HHMM/backup_log.txt backup_log.txt lists every file copied and anything that failed. Nothing is written on the Raspberry Pi itself. Restore from USB Started from Rosetta Settings' File menu > Restore from USB... (window RestoreWindow in Rosetta_Display/Rosetta_Backup.py). Choose the stick, then the backup - the dated folders under Rosetta_Backup/ on the stick, newest first. Older backups made before the renaming (in REB_Backup/ ) are listed too; their old names (REBset_v1.ini, REB_Custom/REB_Custom.hal, REB_Custom/REB_Tool.tbl) are restored under the new ones. The window says how many files of each kind the backup holds, and, in red, any difference between the backup's machine setup and this one's - which role is on each channel, and whether the expansion box is connected. This is a warning only. Restore asks for confirmation (Cancel is the default), then copies each file in the backup back to the folder it came from (the table above). The stick is ejected, and Rosetta Settings closes without saving. Rules: It refuses to start while LinuxCNC is running - LinuxCNC saves RosettaSet_v1.ini as it closes, which would undo the restore. Every file it replaces is first copied to /home/reuben/Documents/ Before_Restore/YYYY-MM-DD_HHMM/ , laid out like a backup. To undo a restore, copy those files back. Files on the Raspberry Pi that aren't in the backup are left alone - nothing is deleted. Each file is written beside the old one and then renamed over it, so a failed copy never leaves a half-written file. Rosetta Settings closes afterwards so it can't save the values it had loaded over the restored settings file. If it had unsaved tuning changes, it asks about them before the restore starts. Programs it calls Program What for Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Backup.py The program itself (Python 3, GTK 3). pgrep Checks whether LinuxCNC is running. udisksctl Unmounts and powers off the stick when the backup or restore is finished. It finds the stick by reading /proc/mounts for the desktop's automount folders (/media/... or /run/media/...). Notes for support Stick not seen: it must be mounted by the desktop - check it opens in the file manager. Only drives mounted under /media or /run/media count, so the SD card and network drives never appear. Back up with no program cutting. If LinuxCNC is running the window warns but still allows it: heavy USB copying can disturb the realtime motion timing. Restoring: use Restore from USB (above). To bring back only the settings from a saved copy, Rosetta Settings' Load Settings also works. Stick missing after a backup or restore: it was ejected. Pull it out and plug it back in. The Raspberry Pi's whole SD card is not backed up - a failed card is replaced by reinstalling ( Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh ) and then copying these files back. Not to be confused with an older Rosetta_Backup.sh (deleted 6 August 2026) that backed up a RoseEngineButlerLocal folder."},{"u":"RosettaChangeNetwork.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Rosetta_Change_Network.sh - Mesa Card and Network Setup","h":[["Purpose","Purpose"],["How-it-is-run","How it is run"],["Parameters","Parameters"],["Setting-up-a-card-base-or-expansion","Setting up a card (base or expansion)"],["Programs-it-calls","Programs it calls"],["Files-it-references","Files it references"],["What-it-changes","What it changes"],["Notes-for-support","Notes for support"],["Related-pages","Related pages"]],"x":"Rosetta’s Key Programs: Overview · Rosetta_Launch.sh · Rosetta_Settings.sh · Rosetta_Backup.sh · Rosetta_Update.sh · Rosetta_Change_Network.sh · Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh Purpose Rosetta_Change_Network.sh sets up the private wired network between the Raspberry Pi and the Mesa 7i92 card(s) for the two-box hardware: Device Address Base box Mesa card 10.10.10.10 Expansion box Mesa card 10.10.10.11 Raspberry Pi wired connection (\"Wired Ethernet\") 10.10.10.100 - no gateway or DNS (the Pi's internet stays on Wi-Fi) It can also write the Rosetta firmware to a new card, put the Raspberry Pi back on the old 192.168.1.120 address, and check which cards answer. See Mesa 7i92 for the cards and their W5/W6 jumpers. How it is run From a terminal (there is no desktop icon). Close LinuxCNC first - the script stops if it's running. /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Change_Network.sh [choice] [--dry-run] [--no-shutdown] With no choice it shows a menu. It asks before every change and asks for the reuben password when it needs sudo . Parameters Parameter What it does base Sets up the connected card as the base card (10.10.10.10): checks it answers at its factory address, 192.168.1.121; offers to write the firmware; stores the new address on the card; moves the Pi to 10.10.10.100; then walks through shutting down and moving the card's jumpers. expansion The same for the expansion card (10.10.10.11). pi Moves only the Pi's wired connection to 10.10.10.100, then reports which cards answer. Use it if the Pi restarted part way through, or when the cards are already set up. back Puts the Pi's wired connection back on 192.168.1.120 - to reach a card at its factory address, or to run software from before the two-box design. check Changes nothing. Shows the Pi's wired address and which cards answer. Start here for any card-connection problem. --dry-run Shows every command without running any of them (cards are assumed to answer). --no-shutdown Used by the installer: stops once the address is stored and the Pi moved, without the shutdown and jumper steps (the installer gives those at its end). Menu: 1 = base, 2 = expansion, 3 = pi, 4 = back, 5 = check. Setting up a card (base or expansion) Run it with the Pi still on 192.168.1.x and only the card being set up connected - two cards at the factory address would clash. W5/W6 at the factory setting, card powered on. It checks the card answers at 192.168.1.121. If not, it stops and changes nothing. Write the firmware? - yes for a new card. Store the new address on the card? - yes. Move the Pi to 10.10.10.100? - yes. Shut the Pi down? - yes, then: switch the machine off, move the card's W5/W6 jumpers to the setting the 7i92 manual calls EEPROM, reconnect any card that was unplugged, and switch on. Once the Pi has started, run Rosetta_Change_Network.sh check - the card should answer at its new address. The whole machine is switched off for the jumper change because the Pi and card share one 5V supply: switching the card on while the Pi is running restarts the Pi without a proper shutdown. Programs it calls Program What for mesaflash --readhmid (does a card answer?), --write ... --reload (firmware), --set ip= (store the address) nmcli Changing, starting and deleting the Pi's \"Wired Ethernet\" connection ip , pgrep Reading the Pi's address; checking LinuxCNC isn't running sudo poweroff Shutting down for the jumper change (only when you answer yes) Files it references Rosetta_Setup/MesaCard/ 7i92t_Rosetta.bin - the firmware written to a new card What it changes It creates no files. It changes: The Mesa card's stored address (in the card's EEPROM), and its firmware if asked. The Pi's \"Wired Ethernet\" network connection (stored by NetworkManager, so it survives a restart), with autoconnect priority 10 so the Pi uses that one. If the Pi has spare connections with the same name (from running the installer more than once), it offers to delete them. Notes for support Undo a card: put its W5/W6 jumpers back to the factory setting and it answers at 192.168.1.121 again. Undo the Pi with back . Which address the software uses: at each launch, Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py uses 192.168.1.121 for the base card while the Pi is on 192.168.1.x, and 10.10.10.10 otherwise - so a machine works before and after the move. Expansion Box - Connected needs the 10.10.10.x network; with it ticked on 192.168.1.x, Rosetta refuses to start and says why. \"No card answered at 192.168.1.121\" : check the cable, the card's power, that W5/W6 are at the factory setting, and that the Pi is on 192.168.1.x ( check ). \"Changing the 'Wired Ethernet' connection failed\" : list the connections with nmcli con show . Try --dry-run first when unsure - it shows exactly what would be run. Related pages Mesa 7i92 Hardware Build Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh Updating Rosetta - going back to software from before the two-box design"},{"u":"RosettaInstall.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh - Installer","h":[["Purpose","Purpose"],["How-it-is-run","How it is run"],["Parameters","Parameters"],["What-it-does","What it does"],["Programs-it-calls","Programs it calls"],["Files-it-references","Files it references"],["Files-and-folders-it-creates","Files and folders it creates"],["Notes-for-support","Notes for support"],["Related-pages","Related pages"]],"x":"Rosetta’s Key Programs: Overview · Rosetta_Launch.sh · Rosetta_Settings.sh · Rosetta_Backup.sh · Rosetta_Update.sh · Rosetta_Change_Network.sh · Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh Purpose Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh finishes setting up a new Raspberry Pi for Rosetta: the wired network, the Mesa card, the key files and the extra programs. It is one step of Installation and Setup for Debian 13 Trixie ; it does not install LinuxCNC or download Rosetta - both must already be done. REB_Install_Debian_12_Bookworm.sh is the older installer for Debian 12 (Bookworm) and isn't used for new machines. How it is run Once, on a new machine, from a terminal, as the reuben user (not with sudo - it asks for the password when needed): cd /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup bash Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh Before starting: the repository is at /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta, LinuxCNC is installed, the Pi has internet on Wi-Fi, and the base box's Mesa card is connected and powered with W5/W6 at the factory setting. Parameters None. What it does Wired network - deletes any existing \"Wired Ethernet\" connections and creates one at 192.168.1.120, no gateway or DNS. Mesa card - copies 7i92t_Rosetta.bin, 7i92t_Rosetta.pin and PIN_Rosetta_34.vhd to /usr/lib/firmware/hm2/hostmot2, then runs Rosetta_Change_Network.sh base --no-shutdown (firmware, address 10.10.10.10, Pi moved to 10.10.10.100). Key files - .axisrc, a copy of Rosetta_Update.sh, the gcode folder and the starting RosettaSet_v1.ini (see below). ClamAV (virus scanner), with /home/reuben/ClamAv/SuspiciousFiles. MenuLibre (menu editor - used to add the desktop and menu entries). galculator (calculator). xfce4-screenshooter (screenshots). Claude Code - Node.js 20 from NodeSource, then the Claude Code npm package into ~/.npm-global. Chromium (web browser, from Debian's own packages). Folder shortcuts - runs Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Add_Shortcuts.sh : \"Rosetta\" and \"Gcode\" in the file manager's Places list, and two Turk's head launchers for those folders on the bottom panel, next to the File Manager. Serial number - runs Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Set_Serial_Number.sh , which asks for this machine's serial number (e.g. 4). Press Enter to skip; it is asked again at the next update. Last step - asks to shut down, then: switch off, move the card's W5/W6 jumpers to EEPROM, switch on, and run Rosetta_Change_Network.sh check . Programs it calls nmcli , Rosetta_Change_Network.sh (which uses mesaflash ), cp , mkdir , sudo apt install , curl (NodeSource setup), npm , claude --version , Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Add_Shortcuts.sh (uses xfconf-query ), Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Set_Serial_Number.sh , sudo poweroff . Files it references Rosetta_Setup/MesaCard/ 7i92t_Rosetta.bin , 7i92t_Rosetta.pin , PIN_Rosetta_34.vhd Rosetta_Setup/ axisrc Rosetta_Update.sh Rosetta_Setup/ RosettaSet_v1.ini - the starting settings Files and folders it creates File or folder Notes \"Wired Ethernet\" network connection Moved to 10.10.10.100 by Rosetta_Change_Network.sh. /usr/lib/firmware/hm2/hostmot2/ 7i92t_Rosetta.* , PIN_Rosetta_34.vhd Copies of the card firmware and pin files. /home/reuben/ .axisrc AXIS window size and position. /home/reuben/ Rosetta_Update.sh A copy. Not used by the icons and never updated - ignore it. /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/ gcode/ The operator's G-code folder. /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini This machine's settings, from the starting copy. /home/reuben/ ClamAv/SuspiciousFiles/ Virus scan quarantine folder. /home/reuben/ .npm-global/ , a PATH line added to .bashrc Claude Code. /home/reuben/.config/gtk-3.0/ bookmarks , two launchers on the bottom panel, /home/reuben/.config/Rosetta/ shortcuts-added The folder shortcuts. The marker file stops them being added again - delete it, or run Rosetta_Add_Shortcuts.sh --force , to add them again. /home/reuben/.config/Rosetta/ serial The serial number typed in (only if one was given). Notes for support Settings file ownership on older machines: installers before 24 October 2026 copied RosettaSet_v1.ini (and the home-folder Rosetta_Update.sh) with sudo, so on a machine installed before then they may have belonged to root. Rosetta now replaces the settings file each time it saves it, which gives it to reuben, so this normally fixes itself. If a save still fails, check the owner and fix it with: ls -l /home/reuben/Documents/RosettaSet_v1.ini sudo chown reuben:reuben /home/reuben/Documents/RosettaSet_v1.ini Running it again is mostly safe: it replaces the \"Wired Ethernet\" connection (at 192.168.1.120 - run Rosetta_Change_Network.sh pi afterwards if the cards are already on 10.10.10.x), and overwrites RosettaSet_v1.ini with the starting settings - back it up first. \"File exists\" messages for folders are harmless. A failed step stops the installer with \"PROGRAM TERMINATED PREMATURELY\". Read the message above it, fix the cause, and run it again. Desktop and menu entries aren't made by the installer - add them with MenuLibre (each icon's Command is the full path of the Rosetta_x.sh script it starts). The expansion box's card is set up separately, with Rosetta_Change_Network.sh expansion . Folder shortcuts or serial number missing: both steps are repeated by every update until they are done, so running Update Rosetta from the desktop fixes them. The shortcuts need the desktop to be running (they can't be added over ssh). Related pages Installation and Setup for Debian 13 Trixie Installation and Checkout List Rosetta_Change_Network.sh"},{"u":"RosettaKeyPrograms.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Rosetta's Key Programs","h":[["Running-any-of-them-from-a-terminal","Running any of them from a terminal"],["Rules-they-share","Rules they share"],["Other-programs-support-may-meet","Other programs support may meet"],["Related-pages","Related pages"]],"x":"Rosetta’s Key Programs: Overview · Rosetta_Launch.sh · Rosetta_Settings.sh · Rosetta_Backup.sh · Rosetta_Update.sh · Rosetta_Change_Network.sh · Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh The Rosetta_x.sh scripts are how the operator - and support - start every part of Rosetta. Each one is short; most of them only start a Python program, which does the real work. All of them live in /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta (the installer is in its Rosetta_Setup folder). Script Started from What it does Main program Rosetta_Launch.sh Rosetta icon Builds this machine's configuration, then starts LinuxCNC. Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py, linuxcnc Rosetta_Settings.sh Rosetta Settings icon Changes this machine's setup and tuning (LinuxCNC closed). Its File menu also has Back Up to USB and Restore from USB. Rosetta_Settings.py Rosetta_Backup.sh Backup Rosetta icon Copies settings, G-code, tool table and custom HAL to a USB stick. (Restore from USB, which copies a backup back, is in Rosetta Settings' File menu.) Rosetta_Backup.py Rosetta_Update.sh Update Rosetta icon Latest files from GitHub, new settings, system updates. git, Rosetta_Update_Settings.py, apt Rosetta_Change_Network.sh Terminal (support) Sets up the Mesa cards' addresses and the Pi's wired network; check shows which cards answer. mesaflash, nmcli Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh Terminal, once (new machine) Network, Mesa card, key files, extra programs, folder shortcuts and serial number. nmcli, Rosetta_Change_Network.sh, apt Running any of them from a terminal The desktop icons hide everything the programs print. When something doesn't work, run the same script in a terminal window and read the messages: cd /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta ./Rosetta_Launch.sh \"Permission denied\" means the script isn't marked as runnable (a script can lose it when it is copied by hand): run it as bash ./Rosetta_Launch.sh , or restore the permission with chmod +x Rosetta_*.sh . Rules they share One at a time. Rosetta Settings, Restore from USB and the network script refuse to run while LinuxCNC is running; close LinuxCNC before an update too. Settings live in one file: /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini . Rosetta Settings writes it, LinuxCNC saves live values into it at shutdown, the update adds new settings to it (and removes ones no longer used), Back Up to USB copies it, and Restore from USB puts a backed-up copy in its place. Rosetta_Launch.sh only reads it. Changes apply at the next launch. Nothing changed in Rosetta Settings, by an update or by the network script reaches a running LinuxCNC. Don't edit the scripts. An update replaces them, and changes to them aren't supported. Machine-specific HAL additions belong in Rosetta_Custom/Rosetta_Custom.hal, and AXIS window settings in /home/reuben/.axisrc. Other programs support may meet These are started by the scripts above or by LinuxCNC, not by the operator: Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py - builds Rosetta.local.ini and the .local HAL files at every launch (see Rosetta_Launch.sh ). Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Update_Settings.py - adds new settings, and removes ones no longer used, during an update (see Rosetta_Update.sh ). Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Renaming.py - at the end of every update, moves a machine still using the old Rose Engine Butler names over to the Rosetta names. Does nothing on a machine already moved. Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_main.py - the AXIS side panel (Indexing, Sync Move, Threading). Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Axis_Commands.py - extra AXIS commands, loaded by AXIS as it starts (Rosetta.ini's [DISPLAY]USER_COMMAND_FILE). It runs /home/reuben/.axisrc, then adds the \"not enabled\" warning before Run (see Rosetta_Launch.sh ). Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Settings_Restore.py - puts the saved scale, PID and limits onto the HAL pins as LinuxCNC starts. Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Scale_Persist.py - saves the live values to RosettaSet_v1.ini at shutdown (run by Rosetta_Shutdown.hal). Rosetta_Display/ rosetta_settings_io.py - shared code that reads and writes RosettaSet_v1.ini for all of the above. Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Backup.py - the Back Up to USB and Restore from USB windows (see Rosetta_Backup.sh ). Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Program_Estimate.py - started by the side panel when a G-code program starts, to work out the progress bar's percentages. Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Add_Shortcuts.sh - adds the Rosetta and Gcode folder shortcuts, once (installer, then updates). Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Set_Serial_Number.sh - asks for and records this machine's serial number in ~/.config/Rosetta/serial (installer, then updates until one is given). Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Report_Version.py - sends the version report at every update (see Rosetta_Update.sh ). Rosetta_Checkout.sh - bench checkout of a newly built system, with a test motor on every channel. Run from a terminal by whoever builds the machine. Related pages Launching Rosetta Updating Rosetta Files & Directories"},{"u":"RosettaLaunch.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Rosetta_Launch.sh - Start Rosetta","h":[["Purpose","Purpose"],["How-it-is-run","How it is run"],["Parameters","Parameters"],["What-it-does","What it does"],["Programs-it-calls","Programs it calls"],["Files-it-reads","Files it reads"],["Files-it-creates","Files it creates"],["Notes-for-support","Notes for support"],["Related-pages","Related pages"]],"x":"Rosetta’s Key Programs: Overview · Rosetta_Launch.sh · Rosetta_Settings.sh · Rosetta_Backup.sh · Rosetta_Update.sh · Rosetta_Change_Network.sh · Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh Purpose Rosetta_Launch.sh starts a Rosetta session. It first builds this machine's own LinuxCNC configuration from the shipped templates and this machine's settings file, then starts LinuxCNC on that generated configuration. It exists so that the shipped files (Rosetta.ini, Rosetta.hal, Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.hal) are never changed on the machine. An update can then replace them without wiping out this machine's own choices (axis assignments, jog speeds, measurement system, tuning, accessories). See Launching Rosetta for the full start-up sequence. How it is run The Rosetta desktop icon, and Applications > Rosetta. From a terminal, which also shows everything it prints: /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Launch.sh Parameters None. Anything typed after the name is ignored. What it does Runs python3 Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py . Before building anything, it checks the gcode folder for reserved file names (see below). If that fails, it stops there (the script uses set -e ), so LinuxCNC never starts on a half-built configuration. Otherwise it replaces itself with linuxcnc Rosetta.local.ini . Programs it calls Program What for Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Generate_Local_Ini.py Builds the .local files below. Uses Rosetta_Display/ rosetta_settings_io.py to read the settings file, and runs ip -4 addr show dev eth0 to choose the base card's address (192.168.1.121 while the Raspberry Pi is still on 192.168.1.x, otherwise 10.10.10.10). linuxcnc Started on Rosetta.local.ini. LinuxCNC in turn loads the HAL files, the AXIS screen with the Rosetta side panel (Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_main.py ), Rosetta_Settings_Restore.py (puts the saved scale, PID and limits onto the HAL pins), and at shutdown Rosetta_Shutdown.hal , which runs Rosetta_Scale_Persist.py . AXIS also runs Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Axis_Commands.py as it starts (see the notes below). Files it reads All paths are under /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta unless shown in full. Rosetta.ini - the configuration template Rosetta.hal - the realtime HAL template Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.hal - the side-panel HAL template Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Splash.ngc - the empty start-up program (used when X and Z aren't both assigned) gcode/ - only the file names, for the reserved-name check /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini - this machine's settings /home/reuben/ .axisrc - AXIS window size (read by AXIS, through Rosetta_Axis_Commands.py) Files it creates These are rewritten at every launch. Never edit them - change the settings (Rosetta Settings) or the templates instead. File Contents Rosetta.local.ini Rosetta.ini with this machine's axis assignment, units, speed limits, jog speeds and backlash, pointing at the files below. This is the file LinuxCNC runs on. Rosetta.local.hal Rosetta.hal with the Mesa card line for one or both cards, each role on its assigned channel, and the limit jacks, E-stop button, signal tower and lost-card E-stop wired in. Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.local.hal Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.hal plus the sound and signal-tower lines. Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Splash.local.ngc The \"Rosetta / ROSETTA\" drawing shown in the preview at start-up. Preview only - pressing Run skips every move in it. LinuxCNC itself also writes sim.var (G-code parameters) while it runs, and at shutdown Rosetta_Scale_Persist.py saves the live values back into RosettaSet_v1.ini . Notes for support \"Reserved file name found - startup stopped\": the gcode folder holds a file whose name starts with Rosetta (any case) or is M100 - M199 . LinuxCNC looks in that folder first, so such a file would run in place of Rosetta's own subroutines. Rename it (the Gcode folder shortcut opens the folder), then start again. A short sound plays when startup is stopped. Rosetta Settings' Other tab has a Startup File Check setting: Abort startup - recommended (the default) or Warn, but let me continue . Nothing happens when the icon is clicked: run Rosetta_Launch.sh from a terminal. The desktop icon hides the output; the terminal shows the generator's messages and any error. The generator explains itself. It prints which cards it loads and at which addresses, which role is on which channel, and which limit jacks, E-stop and signal tower are wired. If it finds a problem it prints REFUSING to write Rosetta.local.hal with the reason, and LinuxCNC doesn't start. Changes need a restart. Everything Rosetta.local.ini sets is read once when LinuxCNC starts, so a change made in Rosetta Settings applies at the next launch. Card can't be found: check the Raspberry Pi's wired address with Rosetta_Change_Network.sh check (see Rosetta_Change_Network.sh ). With the cable unplugged, the generator prints a note and uses 10.10.10.10. Expansion Box - Connected must match the hardware. Ticked with no expansion card answering, LinuxCNC stops with a card error. To compare what was generated with the template, open Rosetta.local.hal and Rosetta.hal side by side. Rosetta_Axis_Commands.py and .axisrc: Rosetta.ini names Rosetta_Axis_Commands.py as AXIS's [DISPLAY]USER_COMMAND_FILE. With that set, AXIS no longer reads /home/reuben/.axisrc itself, so Rosetta_Axis_Commands.py runs it first. If .axisrc has a mistake, AXIS shows an Error in ~/.axisrc box and carries on. \"X not enabled\" box when clicking Run (since 08 November 2026): the loaded program moves an axis whose ENA button is off - it would stop with a following error as soon as that axis had to move. Cancel (the default) and turn the ENA on, or Run Anyway. The side panel's index, Sync Move and Threading buttons give the same warning (with Move Anyway), and also check the spindles. The check uses AXIS's preview, so a rapid move before the program's first feed move isn't seen. Related pages Launching Rosetta Axis Mapping Files & Directories"},{"u":"RosettaSettings.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Rosetta_Settings.sh - Rosetta Settings Program","h":[["Purpose","Purpose"],["How-it-is-run","How it is run"],["Parameters","Parameters"],["File-menu","File menu"],["Programs-it-calls","Programs it calls"],["Files-it-reads","Files it reads"],["Files-it-creates-or-changes","Files it creates or changes"],["Notes-for-support","Notes for support"],["Related-pages","Related pages"]],"x":"Rosetta’s Key Programs: Overview · Rosetta_Launch.sh · Rosetta_Settings.sh · Rosetta_Backup.sh · Rosetta_Update.sh · Rosetta_Change_Network.sh · Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh Purpose Rosetta_Settings.sh starts Rosetta Settings , the window where this machine's setup is changed: which axis or spindle is on each channel, the expansion box, limit jacks, E-stop button, signal tower, sounds, scale, speed limits, backlash, PID tuning, jog speeds, measurement system and device names. Everything it changes is stored in /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini and takes effect the next time Rosetta is started. How to use each page is in the User Manual (web site); this page covers what support needs to know. How it is run The Rosetta Settings (gear) desktop icon, and Applications > Rosetta. From a terminal, which also shows its messages: /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Settings.sh LinuxCNC must be closed first. If it's running, Rosetta Settings shows \"LinuxCNC is currently running\" and closes. Parameters None. File menu Save All Settings (Ctrl+S), Save All Settings As... (Shift+Ctrl+S), Save Subset of Settings As... , Load Settings from File... (Ctrl+O) - the same as the four buttons on the Axis Selection tab. Back Up to USB... - opens the same window as the Back Up to USB icon. Restore from USB... - copies a backup from a USB stick back onto the Raspberry Pi (see Rosetta_Backup.sh ), then closes Rosetta Settings. Quit (Ctrl+Q) - asks about unsaved tuning changes. Programs it calls Program What for Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Settings.py The program itself (Python 3, GTK 3). Uses Rosetta_Display/ rosetta_settings_io.py to read and write the settings file. halcmd Reads and sets live HAL values. Since LinuxCNC isn't running while Rosetta Settings is open, these calls normally find nothing; \"halcmd not found\" or \"pin not found\" messages in the terminal are harmless. The web browser The User Manual buttons open the matching User Manual page. Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Backup.py The File menu's Back Up to USB and Restore from USB windows. Files it reads Rosetta_Display/ Rosetta_Settings_v1.ui - the window layout /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini - this machine's settings Any settings file chosen with Load Settings (normally in /home/reuben/Documents) Files it creates or changes File When /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini As soon as they change: channel assignments, expansion box, limit jacks and switch type, E-stop button, signal tower, sounds, startup file check, backlash, jog speeds, measurement system, device names. Only on Save All Settings: scale, Max Speed, Max Acceleration, stepgen limits, PID values. Closing the window with unsaved tuning asks Save and Close / Close Without Saving / Cancel. A file name chosen by the operator, normally in /home/reuben/Documents Save All Settings As... (a full copy) and Save Subset (an export of chosen settings, to bring into another machine). Files restored by Restore from USB See Rosetta_Backup.sh . RosettaSet_v1.ini is JSON with comment lines at the top and bottom, despite its .ini name. Don't edit it by hand - use Rosetta Settings, or Load Settings from a saved copy. Notes for support A change \"didn't work\": changes apply at the next launch of Rosetta, not straight away. Ask whether LinuxCNC was restarted. Tuning changes lost: scale, speed limits and PID values are only kept after Save All Settings (Ctrl+S). Duplicate! / Conflict! warnings (red) mean that change has not been saved - two channels have the same role, Sp1 is assigned without Sp0, or two limit jacks clash. Fix the warning and the setting saves. Orange \"not assigned - cleared\" next to a limit jack means its axis lost its channel, so the jack was set to Not used. Expansion Box - Connected needs the 10.10.10.x network - see Rosetta_Change_Network.sh . LinuxCNC also saves the live scale and other values into RosettaSet_v1.ini when it shuts down, so values tuned in a running session end up in the file too. Startup File Check (Other tab) decides what happens when the gcode folder holds a reserved file name - see Rosetta_Launch.sh . Leave it at Abort startup - recommended . Before trying a different set of settings, take a copy with Save All Settings As... - it can be loaded back with Load Settings. Rosetta Settings closed by itself after Restore from USB - that is deliberate, so it can't save the settings it was showing over the restored ones. Open it again to see the restored settings. Related pages Axis Mapping Panel Accessories PID Tuning Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning (User Manual)"},{"u":"RosettaUpdate.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Rosetta_Update.sh - Update Rosetta","h":[["Purpose","Purpose"],["How-it-is-run","How it is run"],["Parameters","Parameters"],["What-it-does","What it does"],["Programs-it-calls","Programs it calls"],["Files-it-reads","Files it reads"],["Files-it-creates-or-changes","Files it creates or changes"],["Notes-for-support","Notes for support"]],"x":"Rosetta’s Key Programs: Overview · Rosetta_Launch.sh · Rosetta_Settings.sh · Rosetta_Backup.sh · Rosetta_Update.sh · Rosetta_Change_Network.sh · Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh Purpose Rosetta_Update.sh brings a machine up to date: the latest Rosetta files from GitHub, any new settings those files need, and the latest Debian, LinuxCNC and ClamAV packages. This machine's own settings and tuning are kept. Updating Rosetta covers local changes, branches and going back to an earlier version; this page covers the program itself. How it is run The Update Rosetta desktop icon, and Applications > Rosetta > Update Rosetta. It runs in a terminal window. From a terminal: bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Update.sh Close LinuxCNC and Rosetta Settings first. It asks for the reuben password (for sudo ). It needs the internet (Wi-Fi). Machines installed from the instructions get the files over HTTPS and need no GitHub login; a machine whose repository was switched to SSH ( git remote -v shows git@github.com) also needs the reuben user's SSH key registered with GitHub. Parameters None. What it does git stash - sets aside local changes to files in the repository (e.g. the tool table). git pull - gets the latest version of the branch the machine is on (normally main). If this fails, it puts the local changes back and stops. git stash pop - puts the local changes back. If the update changed the same lines, it leaves the files exactly as pulled, keeps the local changes in the stash, and prints a warning. Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Update_Settings.py - adds any new settings to this machine's settings file. A problem here is reported but the update carries on. It then: runs the one-time steps for machines built before they were added to the installer - Rosetta_Add_Shortcuts.sh (folder shortcuts) and Rosetta_Set_Serial_Number.sh (asks for the serial number if none is recorded). Each does nothing once it has been done. sends a version report (see below). At the end, Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Renaming.py moves a machine still using the old Rose Engine Butler names over to the Rosetta names (it does nothing on a machine already moved). Refreshes the EtherLab package signing key - only if that package source is set up; a failure only warns. sudo apt update and sudo apt upgrade -y - stops with an error if either fails. Programs it calls Program What for git (stash, pull, stash pop, reset) Getting the latest files from GitHub, keeping local edits. Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Update_Settings.py Adds new settings to RosettaSet_v1.ini; uses Rosetta_Display/ rosetta_settings_io.py . Rosetta_Setup/ Rosetta_Add_Shortcuts.sh , Rosetta_Set_Serial_Number.sh , Rosetta_Report_Version.py The one-time steps and the version report, run by Rosetta_Update_Settings.py. curl , gpg , cmp , sudo install Fetching, checking and installing the EtherLab signing key. sudo apt update , sudo apt upgrade -y Updating Debian, LinuxCNC, ClamAV and the other packages. Files it reads The whole repository at /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta (through git) Rosetta_Setup/ RosettaSet_v1.ini - the shipped starting settings, with every setting /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini - this machine's settings /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ ethercat.sources - the EtherLab package source, if present Files it creates or changes File Change The repository files Replaced with the latest versions. Local edits are kept in a stash named Rosetta_Update date time when they can't be put back. /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini New settings added with their starting values; Max Speed / Max Acceleration / PID Max Output filled in from this machine's own values if missing. Existing values are never changed, except: - a stepgen speed or acceleration limit is raised if it is now below the axis's Max Speed / Max Acceleration; - a spindle still on the old indexing PID (exactly P 2, I 1, D 1.2) gets I 0 and D 0 (since 08 November 2026 - the old values overshot and crept back); - settings no longer used are removed: channel_types , signal_tower_active_low and the spindles' pid_vel (since 09 November 2026). Missing file: created from the shipped copy. /home/reuben/Documents/ RosettaSet_v1.ini.bak- YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS A copy of the settings file from before the change - only when something was added, changed or removed. Other /home/reuben/Documents/ *.ini settings files The explanatory comment lines are added to saved and exported settings files that lack them. Their settings aren't changed. The EtherLab keyring named in ethercat.sources Replaced, only if a newer copy of the same key is available. /home/reuben/.config/Rosetta/ report_log.jsonl One line added for every version report, whether or not it could be sent. /home/reuben/.config/Rosetta/ serial , the folder shortcuts Only the first time, on a machine that doesn't have them yet. System packages Updated by apt. Notes for support It updates whichever branch the machine is on. Check with git branch --show-current - customer machines should be on main . \"git pull failed\" - usually no internet. On a machine using SSH ( git remote -v ), GitHub may be asking for a passphrase or rejecting the key - test it with ssh -T git@github.com as reuben. \"local changes could not be re-applied\" - see Updating Rosetta for finding them in the stash. \"settings file could not be checked\" - RosettaSet_v1.ini couldn't be read. It is left as it was; check it isn't empty or hand-edited, and that reuben owns it ( ls -l ~/Documents/RosettaSet_v1.ini ). EtherLab key warnings are harmless - nothing Rosetta uses comes from that source. Version report. Every update sends this machine's Raspberry Pi hardware serial number, its serial number (if one was recorded), the Rosetta version and date shown on the panel, and the time, to https://rosetta.colvintools.com/Support/reb_report.php . Nothing else is sent. It is logged first in ~/.config/Rosetta/report_log.jsonl; if there's no internet it prints a warning and the update carries on (that report isn't sent later). It asks for a serial number at every update until one is given (press Enter to skip). To set it later, run bash /home/reuben/linuxcnc/configs/Rosetta/Rosetta_Setup/Rosetta_Set_Serial_Number.sh . Run it twice after a big change: the first run may update Rosetta_Update.sh or Rosetta_Update_Settings.py themselves, and the second run uses the new versions. Once the update has run, start Rosetta normally - the new files take effect at the next launch. The installer also puts a copy of this script at /home/reuben/ Rosetta_Update.sh . Nothing uses it - the desktop icon and menu entry run the one in the Rosetta folder - and updates never refresh it, so don't run it."},{"u":"SignalTowerRelays.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Signal Tower Relays","h":[["Key-Notes","Key Notes"]],"x":"Signal Tower Relay Connections Key Notes The connections to the Mesa 7i92 is based on the Mesa 7i92TH Pin Mapping . This is based on a relay operating at +5 VDC. There are other relays which operate at +3 to 3.3 VDC, or other voltages. The red VCC line will need to be adjusted to match the relay’s voltage requirements."},{"u":"UpdatingRosetta.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Signal Tower Relays","h":[["Key-Notes","Key Notes"]],"x":"Signal Tower Relay Connections Key Notes The connections to the Mesa 7i92 is based on the Mesa 7i92TH Pin Mapping . This is based on a relay operating at +5 VDC. There are other relays which operate at +3 to 3.3 VDC, or other voltages. The red VCC line will need to be adjusted to match the relay’s voltage requirements."},{"u":"VirusScanning.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Virus Scanning","h":[["Key-Notes","Key Notes"],["Installation","Installation"],["Signature-Updates","Signature Updates"],["Scanning-a-USB-Stick","Scanning a USB Stick"],["Scheduling-the-Weekly-Scan","Scheduling the Weekly Scan"],["Machines-set-up-with-the-earlier-instructions","Machines set up with the earlier instructions"],["Checking-the-Results","Checking the Results"],["Scanning-the-Whole-System","Scanning the Whole System"]],"x":"Key Notes Rosetta uses ClamAV (web site), the scanner most often recommended for a Raspberry Pi. Files mostly arrive on a Rosetta from USB sticks (G-code, settings) and web downloads, so those are what the regular scan covers. The installer ( Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh ) installs ClamAV and creates /home/reuben/ClamAv/SuspiciousFiles. ClamAV keeps its virus signatures up to date by itself (below) - nothing needs scheduling for that. A scan uses a lot of the Raspberry Pi's processor and memory (about 1 GB for the signatures). Never scan while a program is cutting - the weekly scan below skips itself if LinuxCNC is running. Installation Done by the installer. By hand: sudo apt install -y clamav mkdir -p /home/reuben/ClamAv/SuspiciousFiles Installing clamav also installs clamav-freshclam , a background service that checks for new signatures several times a day. Signature Updates Check that the update service is running, and when it last updated: systemctl status clamav-freshclam sudo tail -n 20 /var/log/clamav/freshclam.log Don't run sudo freshclam by hand while the service is running - the two lock each other out and freshclam fails with “Failed to lock the log file”. To force an update now: sudo systemctl stop clamav-freshclam sudo freshclam sudo systemctl start clamav-freshclam Scanning a USB Stick Before copying files from a USB stick, insert it, wait for it to appear on the desktop, then: clamscan -r -i /media/reuben -i lists only infected files. The last lines give a summary - “Infected files: 0” means nothing was found. Scheduling the Weekly Scan This scans the operator's files and any inserted USB stick each Monday at 03:00, at the lowest priority, and skips the scan if LinuxCNC is running. Files found are moved to /home/reuben/ClamAv/SuspiciousFiles. Open a terminal emulator, and enter this command (it edits root's schedule, so the scan doesn't need sudo): sudo crontab -e Choose option 1 if asked. At the bottom of the file, add this line (it is a single line): 0 3 * * 1 pgrep -x linuxcncsvr >/dev/null || nice -n 19 ionice -c3 clamscan -r -i --exclude-dir=/home/reuben/ClamAv --move=/home/reuben/ClamAv/SuspiciousFiles --log=/home/reuben/ClamAv/ScanLog.txt /home/reuben /media Press Ctrl-O then Enter to save, and Ctrl-X to exit. The scan only runs if the machine is switched on at that time. Machines set up with the earlier instructions Earlier instructions scheduled ClamAV differently. Remove those entries - they either fail or scan the whole system with --move, which could move a system file: Applications > Settings > Session and Startup > Application Autostart : select ClamAV (sudo nice -n 19 freshclam) and click Remove . Run crontab -e (without sudo) and delete the two lines with freshclam and clamscan in them. Then add the line above with sudo crontab -e . Checking the Results sudo tail -n 30 /home/reuben/ClamAv/ScanLog.txt sudo grep FOUND /home/reuben/ClamAv/ScanLog.txt ls -l /home/reuben/ClamAv/SuspiciousFiles Each run adds a section to ScanLog.txt ending with a summary. Lines ending in FOUND name a file that was detected and moved. A file was moved: it is in /home/reuben/ClamAv/SuspiciousFiles. If it is a file the customer recognises and trusts (e.g. their own G-code - a false alarm is possible), move it back with sudo mv . Otherwise delete it, and check where it came from (usually a USB stick - scan that stick on another computer too). Nothing has run: check the cron line with sudo crontab -l , and that the machine was on and LinuxCNC closed on Monday at 03:00. Scanning the Whole System Only occasionally, with LinuxCNC closed - it takes hours. This one only reports (no --move), because moving a system file because of a false alarm could stop the Raspberry Pi from starting: sudo nice -n 19 ionice -c3 clamscan -r -i --exclude-dir=\"^/(proc|sys|dev|run)\" --log=/home/reuben/ClamAv/FullScanLog.txt / Report anything it finds before deleting it."},{"u":"index.html","m":"Support Manual","t":"Advanced Support Topics","h":[],"x":"This is the Support Manual for the Rosetta system which outlines more technical support topics. It is not recommended as the starting place for the general user. Data which is available in the User Manual is not duplicated here (to ensure there are no inconsistencies). 3 3D Printable Parts 3D Printable Parts in the User Manual A Axis GUI (main system GUI for using LinuxCNC; web site) Axis Mapping (HAL to hardware) B Building a New System Setting up the hardware (base box and optional expansion box) Hardware Parts Common build steps Building with the Rosetta PCB Building without the Rosetta PCB (i.e., using breakout boards) Installing and configuring the software Debian 13 Trixie Installation and Checkout List D Data Types Diagnostics Diagnostic Tools Stepper Diagnostics (web site) Directories - see Files & Directories DM542T Digital Stepper Drive HAL Settings When Using the DM542T Stepper Online DM542T User Manual (PDF) E Enhancement Requests Tracker (GitHub site) Expansion Box - see Hardware Build E-stop (button, or a box losing contact) - see Panel Accessories and Field Troubleshooting F Files & Directories Fonts Used G GitHub for Rosetta (web site) Enhancement Requests Tracker (GitHub site) Issues Tracker (web site) GladeVCP Data Types Display Files - see Files & Directories H HAL File Axis Mapping (HAL to hardware) Data Types PID Tuning Support Programs I Issues Tracker (web site) L Launching Rosetta Lead Screw Configuration Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning (User Manual) Scale Settings for a Device (Leadscrews, Gearing, and the Scale Calculation) (User Manual) Licensing LinuxCNC Documentation (web site) M Machine Tracker Rosetta O/S Version Report ( user ID & password required ) Manuals (Hardware) Mean Well LRS-350 Switching Power Supply Datasheet (PDF) Installation Manual (PDF) Mesa 7i92 Ethernet Anything I/O Card MesaFlash Help (web site) User Manual (PDF) Raspberry Pi System Documentation (web site) StepperOnline DM542T Stepper Drive User Manual (PDF) Manuals (Software) LinuxCNC Documentation (web site) Mesa 7i92TH Ethernet Anything I/O Card Configuring the Mesa 7i92TH Mesa 7i92TH Pin Mapping Network Addresses (base & expansion cards) - see Mesa 7i92 Mesa 7i92 User Manual (PDF) P Panel Accessories PID Tuning R Rosetta’s Key Programs Rosetta_Backup.sh (Back Up to USB) Rosetta_Change_Network.sh (Mesa card and network setup) Rosetta_Install_Debian_13_Trixie.sh (installer) Rosetta_Launch.sh (start Rosetta) Rosetta_Settings.sh (Rosetta Settings) Rosetta_Update.sh (Update Rosetta) S Signal Tower Relays Stepper Motors Configuration Axis and Stepper Motor Tuning (User Manual) Scale Settings for a Device (Leadscrews, Gearing, and the Scale Calculation) (User Manual) PID Tuning Diagnostics (web site) T Troubleshooting Diagnostics Diagnostic Tools Stepper Diagnostics (web site) Troubleshooting in the Field U Updating Rosetta V Virus Scanning"}];
